window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["cloudwatch"]=[{"id":"cloudwatch-f0001","card":"q-cloudwatch-001-purpose-and-audience","sec":"cloudwatch-s002","q":"What is Amazon CloudWatch, and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>CloudWatch is the AWS monitoring and observability service. It collects metrics, logs and events from AWS resources, applications and on-premises servers into one place. It solves the problem of knowing how your systems are behaving and being told when they misbehave, without running your own monitoring stack.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0002","card":"q-cloudwatch-001-purpose-and-audience","sec":"cloudwatch-s002","q":"Which roles use CloudWatch and for what?","a":"<p>DevOps engineers and developers use it to troubleshoot and tune applications. IT managers use dashboards to see service health. Security specialists use logs and alarms to spot unusual activity. All of them read from the same metrics and logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0003","card":"q-cloudwatch-002-four-capabilities","sec":"cloudwatch-s003","q":"What are the main capabilities of CloudWatch?","a":"<p>Monitoring (metrics, logs and events in one view), troubleshooting (linking metrics with logs, and with X-Ray traces), proactive optimization (alarms on thresholds or anomaly bands that trigger actions such as Auto Scaling), and log analytics (Logs Insights queries).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0004","card":"q-cloudwatch-002-four-capabilities","sec":"cloudwatch-s003","q":"How does CloudWatch let you act on a problem instead of only observing it?","a":"<p>Alarms watch a metric against a static threshold or an ML-based anomaly detection band. When the state changes, the alarm can notify through SNS, run an Auto Scaling policy, or trigger an EC2 action. Monitoring becomes automatic remediation or paging.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0005","card":"q-cloudwatch-002-four-capabilities","sec":"cloudwatch-s003","q":"What does it mean that CloudWatch gives \"one consolidated view\", and why does it matter during an incident?","a":"<p>Metrics, logs and traces from many services show up on shared dashboards and can be jumped between. During an incident you can see a latency spike on a graph, then open the logs for the same time window, without switching tools. That shortens time to resolution.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0006","card":"q-cloudwatch-003-benefits","sec":"cloudwatch-s004","q":"What are the practical benefits of using CloudWatch for monitoring?","a":"<p>Faster time to resolution because you can visualize and query logs quickly, better performance because alarms catch problems early, and one place for operational data across services. It is also integrated with most AWS services, so many metrics exist without any setup.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0007","card":"q-cloudwatch-003-benefits","sec":"cloudwatch-s004","q":"What are the trade-offs or limits of relying only on CloudWatch for observability?","a":"<p>Cost grows with custom metrics, high-resolution metrics, log ingestion and query volume. It is AWS-focused, so multi-cloud setups often add a third-party tool. Metric data is aggregated and retained at lower resolution as it ages, so it is not a long-term raw data store (stream it out if you need that).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0008","card":"q-cloudwatch-004-metrics-vs-logs","sec":"cloudwatch-s005","q":"What is the difference between metrics and logs in CloudWatch, and when do you use each?","a":"<p>Metrics are numeric data points over time. They are cheap to store, easy to graph and the right input for alarms. Logs are detailed text records of events. Use metrics to see that something is wrong and how often. Use logs to find out what happened and why.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0009","card":"q-cloudwatch-004-metrics-vs-logs","sec":"cloudwatch-s005","q":"What are the three pillars of observability, and which CloudWatch features map to them?","a":"<p>Metrics (CloudWatch Metrics), logs (CloudWatch Logs and Logs Insights) and traces (AWS X-Ray). You typically alarm on a metric, then read logs and traces to find the cause.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0010","card":"q-cloudwatch-004-metrics-vs-logs","sec":"cloudwatch-s005","q":"A service shows a rising error rate. Do you start with metrics or logs, and why?","a":"<p>Start with the metric to confirm scope and timing (which function, since when, how steep). Then move to logs for the same time window to find the actual exception. The metric answers how often. The logs answer what.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0011","card":"q-cloudwatch-007-what-a-metric-is","sec":"cloudwatch-s008","q":"What is a CloudWatch metric?","a":"<p>A time-ordered set of data points that represents one monitored variable, such as API latency or Lambda invocations. Each data point has a timestamp, a value and usually a unit. A metric is identified by its namespace, name and dimensions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0012","card":"q-cloudwatch-007-what-a-metric-is","sec":"cloudwatch-s008","q":"What is a dimension in CloudWatch, and why does it matter?","a":"<p>A dimension is a name/value pair that is part of a metric's identity, for example <code>FunctionName=myFunc</code>. Each unique combination of dimension values is a separate metric. Dimensions let you slice a metric (by function, by environment, by endpoint). A metric published with different dimensions is not the same metric, so queries must use the exact dimension set.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0013","card":"q-cloudwatch-007-what-a-metric-is","sec":"cloudwatch-s008","q":"How long does CloudWatch keep metric data, and what happens to its resolution?","a":"<p>Data is kept and rolled up as it ages. Points under 60 seconds are kept for 3 hours, 1-minute points for 15 days, 5-minute points for 63 days, and 1-hour points for 455 days (15 months). Older data is only available at coarser granularity. Metrics cannot be deleted, they expire.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0014","card":"q-cloudwatch-007-what-a-metric-is","sec":"cloudwatch-s008","q":"Are CloudWatch metrics global or regional?","a":"<p>Regional. A metric lives in the Region it was published to, and you query it in that Region. Cross-Region views need cross-Region dashboards or explicit queries per Region.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0015","card":"q-cloudwatch-008-namespaces","sec":"cloudwatch-s009","q":"What is a CloudWatch namespace, and what is it for?","a":"<p>A namespace is a container that groups related metrics, for example <code>AWS/Lambda</code>. It keeps metrics from different applications or services from being mixed. Metrics in different namespaces are separate even if the names match.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0016","card":"q-cloudwatch-008-namespaces","sec":"cloudwatch-s009","q":"What is the difference between an AWS namespace and a custom namespace?","a":"<p>AWS services publish into namespaces that start with <code>AWS/</code> (such as <code>AWS/EC2</code>, <code>AWS/ApiGateway</code>). Your own metrics go into a custom namespace that you name. You cannot use the <code>AWS/</code> prefix for custom metrics.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0017","card":"q-cloudwatch-008-namespaces","sec":"cloudwatch-s009","q":"How would you name namespaces and dimensions for your own application's metrics?","a":"<p>Use one namespace per application or domain (for example <code>MyCompany/Checkout</code>) and put things you want to slice by into dimensions (environment, endpoint, method).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0018","card":"q-cloudwatch-009-reading-api-gateway-latency-with-get-met","sec":"cloudwatch-s010","q":"In Boto3, what do <code>Id</code>, <code>MetricStat</code>, <code>Period</code> and <code>Stat</code> mean in a <code>get_metric_data</code> query?","a":"<p><code>Id</code> is your label for the query, used to find its results and to refer to it in metric math. <code>MetricStat</code> says which metric to read: <code>Metric</code> identifies it (namespace, name, dimensions), <code>Period</code> is the bucket size in seconds, and <code>Stat</code> is the statistic computed per bucket (Average, Sum, Maximum, p99 and so on).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>response = cloudwatch.get_metric_data(\n    MetricDataQueries=[\n        {\n            &#x27;Id&#x27;: &#x27;m1&#x27;,\n            &#x27;MetricStat&#x27;: {\n                &#x27;Metric&#x27;: {...},\n                &#x27;Period&#x27;: 60,\n                &#x27;Stat&#x27;: &#x27;Average&#x27;\n            }\n        }\n    ],\n    ...\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0019","card":"q-cloudwatch-009-reading-api-gateway-latency-with-get-met","sec":"cloudwatch-s010","q":"Why use <code>get_metric_data</code> instead of <code>get_metric_statistics</code> in Boto3?","a":"<p><code>get_metric_data</code> can fetch many metrics in one call (up to 500), supports metric math expressions, and supports percentile statistics. <code>get_metric_statistics</code> reads one metric per call, with no math. AWS recommends <code>get_metric_data</code> for most new code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0020","card":"q-cloudwatch-009-reading-api-gateway-latency-with-get-met","sec":"cloudwatch-s010","q":"Why does a Boto3 <code>get_metric_data</code> call for API Gateway latency return nothing even though the API is used?","a":"<p>Common causes: the dimensions do not exactly match the metric (for example wrong <code>ApiName</code> value or a missing <code>Stage</code> dimension that the metric was published with), the wrong namespace or Region, a time range that does not cover recent traffic, or no traffic in that window. Dimensions must match exactly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0021","card":"q-cloudwatch-009-reading-api-gateway-latency-with-get-met","sec":"cloudwatch-s010","q":"Why do you pick <code>Average</code> for latency but <code>Sum</code> for error counts in CloudWatch?","a":"<p>Latency is a measure of how long each request took, so an average (or a percentile such as p99) describes it. Errors are counts of events, so the total per period is the meaningful number. Average of a count metric hides how many errors happened.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0022","card":"q-cloudwatch-009-reading-api-gateway-latency-with-get-met","sec":"cloudwatch-s010","q":"Why is p99 often better than Average for API latency in CloudWatch?","a":"<p>An average hides slow outliers. A p99 shows the latency that 99% of requests stay under, which is closer to what the slowest users experience. In <code>get_metric_data</code> you request it with <code>Stat: 'p99'</code> (an <code>ExtendedStatistic</code> in <code>get_metric_statistics</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0023","card":"q-cloudwatch-010-reading-lambda-errors-with-get-metric-st","sec":"cloudwatch-s011","q":"In Boto3, which arguments of <code>get_metric_statistics</code> are required, and what does this call return?","a":"<p>It returns the total number of errors of one Lambda function in 5-minute buckets over the last hour, under the <code>Datapoints</code> key (unsorted). <code>Namespace</code>, <code>MetricName</code>, <code>StartTime</code>, <code>EndTime</code> and <code>Period</code> are required, plus <code>Statistics</code> (or <code>ExtendedStatistics</code>). <code>Dimensions</code> selects the function. A call that would return more than 1,440 data points is rejected, so widen <code>Period</code> for long ranges.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>response = cloudwatch.get_metric_statistics(\n    Namespace=&#x27;AWS/Lambda&#x27;,\n    MetricName=&#x27;Errors&#x27;,\n    Dimensions=[{&#x27;Name&#x27;: &#x27;FunctionName&#x27;, &#x27;Value&#x27;: &#x27;putItemFunction&#x27;}],\n    StartTime=datetime.utcnow() - timedelta(minutes=60),\n    EndTime=datetime.utcnow(),\n    Period=300,\n    Statistics=[&#x27;Sum&#x27;]\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0024","card":"q-cloudwatch-010-reading-lambda-errors-with-get-metric-st","sec":"cloudwatch-s011","q":"In CloudWatch, what is the <code>Errors</code> metric for Lambda, and how does it differ from <code>Invocations</code>?","a":"<p><code>Invocations</code> counts how many times the function was run. <code>Errors</code> counts invocations that ended with a function error (an unhandled exception or a timeout). Error rate is <code>Errors / Invocations</code>, which you can compute with metric math.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0025","card":"q-cloudwatch-010-reading-lambda-errors-with-get-metric-st","sec":"cloudwatch-s011","q":"Which Lambda metrics would you check first for a function that is failing or slow?","a":"<p><code>Errors</code>, <code>Invocations</code>, <code>Duration</code> (check the maximum and p99), <code>Throttles</code>, and <code>ConcurrentExecutions</code>. For async invocations also <code>AsyncEventsDropped</code> and the dead-letter metrics.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0026","card":"q-cloudwatch-011-why-custom-metrics","sec":"cloudwatch-s012","q":"Why would you publish custom metrics to CloudWatch when AWS already provides built-in ones?","a":"<p>Built-in metrics describe infrastructure (CPU, invocations, latency). They do not know your business, such as orders processed, queue wait time or checkout failures. Custom metrics let you graph, alarm on and autoscale from application-level signals, next to the built-in ones on the same dashboard.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0027","card":"q-cloudwatch-011-why-custom-metrics","sec":"cloudwatch-s012","q":"What are the ways to publish a custom metric to CloudWatch?","a":"<p>The <code>PutMetricData</code> API through an SDK (Boto3, AWS SDK for JavaScript), the AWS CLI <code>put-metric-data</code>, the CloudWatch agent (StatsD or collectd), the embedded metric format (EMF) in log lines, or a metric filter that turns log events into a metric.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0028","card":"q-cloudwatch-011-why-custom-metrics","sec":"cloudwatch-s012","q":"When would you use embedded metric format (EMF) instead of calling <code>PutMetricData</code> directly?","a":"<p>In Lambda or other high-volume code. You write a structured JSON log line and CloudWatch extracts metrics from it, so there is no synchronous API call on the request path, no extra latency, and no API throttling concern. You also keep the full event in the logs for debugging.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0029","card":"q-cloudwatch-011-why-custom-metrics","sec":"cloudwatch-s012","q":"What is the cost trade-off of custom metrics?","a":"<p>Each unique metric (namespace, name and dimension combination) is billed per month, and API calls to publish are billed too. High-cardinality dimensions such as user ID or request ID create many metrics and a big bill. Use few, meaningful dimensions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0030","card":"q-cloudwatch-012-resolution","sec":"cloudwatch-s013","q":"What is the difference between standard and high resolution metrics in CloudWatch?","a":"<p>Standard resolution stores data at 1-minute granularity and is the default. High resolution stores data at 1-second granularity. You request it by setting <code>StorageResolution</code> to 1 when publishing. High resolution costs more.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0031","card":"q-cloudwatch-012-resolution","sec":"cloudwatch-s013","q":"When should you use high resolution custom metrics?","a":"<p>When you need to detect short spikes or react fast, for example sub-minute latency bursts or autoscaling signals. Do not use it by default, because it costs more and most dashboards and alarms do not need per-second data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0032","card":"q-cloudwatch-012-resolution","sec":"cloudwatch-s013","q":"In CloudWatch, what is the alarm evaluation period limit for high-resolution metrics?","a":"<p>A high-resolution alarm can evaluate at a period of 10 seconds or 30 seconds, instead of the 60-second minimum for standard metrics. It is billed at a higher rate than a standard alarm.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0033","card":"q-cloudwatch-013-publishing-with-boto3","sec":"cloudwatch-s014","q":"In Boto3, what does each field in this <code>put_metric_data</code> call do?","a":"<p><code>Namespace</code> groups the metric. <code>MetricName</code> names it. <code>Value</code> is the data point. <code>Unit</code> tells CloudWatch and the console how to label and aggregate it. <code>Timestamp</code> is when the value occurred (defaults to the current time if you leave it out). <code>StorageResolution: 1</code> stores the point at 1-second resolution instead of 1 minute.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>cloudwatch.put_metric_data(\n    Namespace=&#x27;MyApp/CustomMetrics&#x27;,\n    MetricData=[\n        {\n            &#x27;MetricName&#x27;: &#x27;OrderProcessingTime&#x27;,\n            &#x27;Value&#x27;: 150.0,\n            &#x27;Unit&#x27;: &#x27;Milliseconds&#x27;,\n            &#x27;Timestamp&#x27;: datetime.utcnow(),\n            &#x27;StorageResolution&#x27;: 1\n        }\n    ]\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0034","card":"q-cloudwatch-013-publishing-with-boto3","sec":"cloudwatch-s014","q":"Why should you set <code>Unit</code> when publishing a custom metric?","a":"<p>The unit shows on graphs, and it lets you request statistics by unit (CloudWatch can return values filtered by the unit). Without a unit the metric is stored as <code>None</code>, and mixing units for the same metric name makes data hard to read.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0035","card":"q-cloudwatch-013-publishing-with-boto3","sec":"cloudwatch-s014","q":"How do you publish many observations of the same metric efficiently with <code>put_metric_data</code>?","a":"<p>Send them in one call as a list in <code>MetricData</code>, or use <code>Values</code> and <code>Counts</code> (or a <code>StatisticValues</code> set) to send a pre-aggregated distribution instead of one call per observation. This reduces API calls, cost and throttling risk.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0036","card":"q-cloudwatch-013-publishing-with-boto3","sec":"cloudwatch-s014","q":"What IAM permission does a program need to publish custom metrics?","a":"<p><code>cloudwatch:PutMetricData</code>. This action does not support resource-level restrictions, so the policy uses <code>Resource: \"*\"</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0037","card":"q-cloudwatch-013-publishing-with-boto3","sec":"cloudwatch-s014","q":"A Boto3 <code>put_metric_data</code> call succeeds, but the metric does not show in the console. What do you check?","a":"<p>Whether you are looking in the same Region as the publisher, the exact namespace and dimension set, and whether you picked the right statistic and time range. New custom metrics can also take a couple of minutes to appear in the metric list.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0038","card":"q-cloudwatch-014-publishing-from-a-node-js-app-setup","sec":"cloudwatch-s015","q":"In the AWS SDK for JavaScript v3, why are packages such as <code>@aws-sdk/client-cloudwatch</code> installed individually?","a":"<p>v3 is modular. Each service has its own client package, so you install only what you use. That keeps bundles smaller and loading faster than v2, which shipped every service in a single <code>aws-sdk</code> package.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0039","card":"q-cloudwatch-014-publishing-from-a-node-js-app-setup","sec":"cloudwatch-s015","q":"Why does a Node.js app that publishes custom metrics use a least-privilege IAM policy with only <code>PutMetricData</code>?","a":"<p>If the credentials leak or the app is compromised, the attacker can only write metric data, not read logs, change alarms or touch other services. Grant the minimum actions the code calls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0040","card":"q-cloudwatch-014-publishing-from-a-node-js-app-setup","sec":"cloudwatch-s015","q":"Where should the AWS credentials for a metric-publishing app live: <code>.env</code> with access keys, or an IAM role?","a":"<p>On AWS compute (EC2, ECS, Lambda), use an IAM role so the SDK gets temporary credentials automatically and nothing is stored. Long-lived access keys in <code>.env</code> are acceptable only for local development, must be kept out of source control, and should be rotated or avoided. Never commit them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0041","card":"q-cloudwatch-015-publishing-from-a-node-js-app-cloudwatch","sec":"cloudwatch-s016","q":"In the AWS SDK for JavaScript v3, what role does <code>fromNodeProviderChain()</code> play here?","a":"<p>It returns a credential provider that walks the standard chain: environment variables, shared credentials/config files and SSO, container or EC2 instance metadata roles, and so on. The same code works on a laptop with keys and on AWS with a role. It is also the default, so passing it explicitly is optional.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>const { fromNodeProviderChain } = require(&#x27;@aws-sdk/credential-provider-node&#x27;);\n\nconst cloudwatchClient = new CloudWatchClient({\n  region: process.env.AWS_REGION,\n  credentials: fromNodeProviderChain()\n});</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0042","card":"q-cloudwatch-015-publishing-from-a-node-js-app-cloudwatch","sec":"cloudwatch-s016","q":"In the AWS SDK for JavaScript v3, how do you call an API such as PutMetricData, and why is it written this way?","a":"<p>v3 uses the command pattern: you build a command object for the operation and pass it to <code>client.send</code>. Importing only the commands you use enables tree shaking, so unused operations are not bundled.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>const command = new PutMetricDataCommand(params);\nawait cloudwatchClient.send(command);</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0043","card":"q-cloudwatch-015-publishing-from-a-node-js-app-cloudwatch","sec":"cloudwatch-s016","q":"Why does the metric publisher wrap <code>send</code> in try/catch and only log the error instead of rethrowing?","a":"<p>Telemetry should not break the business request. If CloudWatch is throttled or unreachable, the app should keep serving users and log the failure. The trade-off is that metrics can be lost silently, so also alert on the publisher's own error logs.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>try {\n  ...\n  await cloudwatchClient.send(command);\n  console.log(&#x27;Successfully published metric to CloudWatch&#x27;);\n} catch (error) {\n  console.error(&#x27;Error publishing metric:&#x27;, error);\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0044","card":"q-cloudwatch-015-publishing-from-a-node-js-app-cloudwatch","sec":"cloudwatch-s016","q":"What is the downside of awaiting <code>PutMetricData</code> inside a user request handler, and what are the alternatives?","a":"<p>It adds a network round trip to every request and couples request latency to CloudWatch availability. Alternatives are batching metrics and flushing on an interval, publishing in the background without blocking the response, using the CloudWatch agent with StatsD, or using embedded metric format in logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0045","card":"q-cloudwatch-016-publishing-from-a-node-js-app-measuring","sec":"cloudwatch-s017","q":"In Node.js, what is wrong with how this code records response time for failed requests?","a":"<p>The metric is published only on success, so failed or timed-out requests are missing from the latency data and averages look better than reality. Publish the duration in a <code>finally</code> block (or in the catch path with an error dimension), and publish a separate error count metric.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>async function makeApiRequest() {\n  const startTime = Date.now();\n  try {\n    const response = await axios.get(process.env.API_ENDPOINT);\n    const responseTime = Date.now() - startTime;\n    ...\n    await publishMetric(&#x27;ResponseTime&#x27;, responseTime, &#x27;Milliseconds&#x27;, dimensions);\n    return response.data;\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.error(&#x27;API request failed:&#x27;, error);\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0046","card":"q-cloudwatch-016-publishing-from-a-node-js-app-measuring","sec":"cloudwatch-s017","q":"Why attach dimensions such as environment, endpoint and HTTP method to a custom latency metric in CloudWatch?","a":"<p>Dimensions let you filter and compare: production versus staging, one endpoint versus another, GET versus POST. The trade-off is that each dimension combination is a separate metric, and a graph for a dimension set only shows metrics published with exactly that set. If you also want a total across endpoints, publish it separately without that dimension.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0047","card":"q-cloudwatch-016-publishing-from-a-node-js-app-measuring","sec":"cloudwatch-s017","q":"What dimension values should you avoid on a custom CloudWatch metric?","a":"<p>Unbounded values such as user ID, request ID, session ID or full URLs with parameters. They create a new metric per value, which is slow to query and expensive. Use bounded categories (route template, status class, environment).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0048","card":"q-cloudwatch-017-viewing-custom-metrics","sec":"cloudwatch-s018","q":"In the CloudWatch console, why might a sparse custom metric look empty on a graph, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>With a long period (such as 5 minutes) and the Average statistic, a few points can be smoothed away or fall outside the time window. Shorten the period (down to 1 second for high-resolution data), use Sum or SampleCount for rarely published values, and widen or move the time range.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0049","card":"q-cloudwatch-017-viewing-custom-metrics","sec":"cloudwatch-s018","q":"Why can you not find the custom metric under its dimension in the console after publishing it with different dimensions?","a":"<p>CloudWatch lists metrics per exact dimension combination. A metric published with <code>Environment</code> and <code>Endpoint</code> appears only under that combination, not under <code>Environment</code> alone. To see an aggregate, publish an extra data point with fewer dimensions, or use metric math or a Metrics Insights <code>GROUP BY</code> query.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0050","card":"q-cloudwatch-018-what-streaming-gives-you","sec":"cloudwatch-s019","q":"What is a CloudWatch metric stream, and why use it?","a":"<p>A metric stream continuously pushes CloudWatch metrics to a destination you choose, through Amazon Data Firehose (formerly Kinesis Data Firehose). You use it to analyze metrics outside CloudWatch: in S3 with Athena, in a data warehouse such as Redshift, or in a third-party observability tool, with low delivery latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0051","card":"q-cloudwatch-018-what-streaming-gives-you","sec":"cloudwatch-s019","q":"Why use a metric stream instead of polling <code>GetMetricData</code> on a schedule?","a":"<p>Polling calls the API repeatedly, which costs money, can be throttled, and adds delay. A stream pushes new data as it arrives, scales with the number of metrics, and does not need a collector that you maintain. Polling is fine for a small, targeted set of metrics.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0052","card":"q-cloudwatch-018-what-streaming-gives-you","sec":"cloudwatch-s019","q":"Which destinations can a CloudWatch metric stream deliver to?","a":"<p>It sends to an Amazon Data Firehose delivery stream, which in turn can write to S3, Redshift, an HTTP endpoint, or supported partners such as Datadog, Splunk or New Relic. CloudWatch itself talks to Firehose only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0053","card":"q-cloudwatch-018-what-streaming-gives-you","sec":"cloudwatch-s019","q":"What output formats does a CloudWatch metric stream support?","a":"<p>JSON and OpenTelemetry (Protobuf) formats. Choose JSON for easy querying with Athena, and OpenTelemetry when the destination expects it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0054","card":"q-cloudwatch-019-creating-a-stream-in-the-console","sec":"cloudwatch-s020","q":"When creating a CloudWatch metric stream, why must a custom Firehose use Direct PUT as its source?","a":"<p>CloudWatch writes records to Firehose by calling its API directly. A Firehose delivery stream that reads from Kinesis Data Streams or another source would not accept those writes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0055","card":"q-cloudwatch-019-creating-a-stream-in-the-console","sec":"cloudwatch-s020","q":"What does the quick S3 setup option create for a metric stream?","a":"<p>It creates the Firehose delivery stream, the S3 bucket and the IAM roles that CloudWatch and Firehose need, so you do not wire them by hand. For production you usually create these yourself to control naming, encryption and retention.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0056","card":"q-cloudwatch-019-creating-a-stream-in-the-console","sec":"cloudwatch-s020","q":"Why would you stream only selected namespaces instead of all metrics?","a":"<p>Cost and noise. Metric streams are billed per metric update, and Firehose and the destination bill for volume. Streaming only the namespaces you analyze, or excluding noisy ones, limits cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0057","card":"q-cloudwatch-020-creating-a-stream-with-boto3","sec":"cloudwatch-s021","q":"In Boto3, what is the role of <code>RoleArn</code> and what must it allow in <code>create_metric_stream</code>?","a":"<p><code>RoleArn</code> is the IAM role that CloudWatch assumes to write into the Firehose delivery stream. It must trust the CloudWatch metric streams service and allow <code>firehose:PutRecord</code> and <code>firehose:PutRecordBatch</code> on that stream. Without it the stream is created but delivery fails.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>response = cloudwatch.create_metric_stream(\n    Name=&#x27;streamdemo&#x27;,\n    IncludeFilters=[{&#x27;Namespace&#x27;: &#x27;CloudWatchApiCourse&#x27;}],\n    FirehoseArn=&#x27;arn:aws:firehose:us-east-1:account-id:deliverystream/metrics-streams-streamdemo&#x27;,\n    RoleArn=&#x27;arn:aws:iam::account-id:role/CloudWatch-Metrics-Stream-Role&#x27;,\n    OutputFormat=&#x27;json&#x27;\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0058","card":"q-cloudwatch-020-creating-a-stream-with-boto3","sec":"cloudwatch-s021","q":"In Boto3 <code>create_metric_stream</code>, what is the difference between <code>IncludeFilters</code> and <code>ExcludeFilters</code>?","a":"<p><code>IncludeFilters</code> streams only the listed namespaces. <code>ExcludeFilters</code> streams everything except the listed namespaces. You can use one or the other, not both. With neither, all metrics are streamed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0059","card":"q-cloudwatch-021-verifying-a-stream","sec":"cloudwatch-s022","q":"You created a CloudWatch metric stream but see nothing in S3. How do you troubleshoot?","a":"<p>Check in order: the stream is in the running state and filters include traffic that exists, the IAM role can write to Firehose, the Firehose delivery stream shows incoming records and no delivery errors, and the Firehose role can write to the bucket. Then wait: Firehose buffers by size and interval, so delivery can lag by a few minutes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0060","card":"q-cloudwatch-021-verifying-a-stream","sec":"cloudwatch-s022","q":"Why does data from a metric stream arrive in S3 in batches instead of instantly?","a":"<p>Firehose buffers records until a size or time threshold is reached, and then writes one object. Lower buffer settings reduce delay but create more, smaller S3 objects, which are less efficient to query.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0061","card":"q-cloudwatch-022-using-streamed-data","sec":"cloudwatch-s023","q":"How would you analyze metrics streamed to S3 in CloudWatch?","a":"<p>Create an Athena table (typically through AWS Glue) over the S3 prefix and query it with SQL. For heavy repeated analysis, convert to Parquet, or load into Redshift. Partitioning by date keeps scans small and cheap.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0062","card":"q-cloudwatch-022-using-streamed-data","sec":"cloudwatch-s023","q":"What are good practices before running a metric stream in production?","a":"<p>Test with one namespace and a small amount of traffic, watch Firehose metrics (incoming records, delivery to S3 success, throttling), set an S3 lifecycle rule to expire old objects, and put an alarm on delivery failures so gaps are noticed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0063","card":"q-cloudwatch-023-running-a-query","sec":"cloudwatch-s024","q":"What is CloudWatch Logs Insights, and when do you use it?","a":"<p>An interactive query service for log data in CloudWatch Logs, with its own pipe-based query language. Use it for ad hoc investigation: finding errors, counting events, grouping by field or time. It queries existing logs, so it works retroactively.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0064","card":"q-cloudwatch-023-running-a-query","sec":"cloudwatch-s024","q":"In CloudWatch Logs Insights, why must you select a log group and a time range before running a query?","a":"<p>Queries run against the chosen log groups over the chosen period, and you are billed on the amount of data scanned. Narrow groups and a tight time range give faster, cheaper queries.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0065","card":"q-cloudwatch-023-running-a-query","sec":"cloudwatch-s024","q":"In CloudWatch Logs Insights, does adding <code>| limit 20</code> reduce the cost of a query?","a":"<p>No. Cost depends on how much log data is scanned, not how many rows are returned. <code>limit</code> only reduces what you see (as of 2025, without a <code>limit</code> a query returns up to 10,000 rows). To reduce cost, shorten the time range, query fewer log groups, and filter early.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0066","card":"q-cloudwatch-023-running-a-query","sec":"cloudwatch-s024","q":"Why do you query several log groups in one Logs Insights query?","a":"<p>To trace one request or error across services (for example API Gateway and Lambda) in a single result, ordered by <code>@timestamp</code>. You can select multiple log groups in the console, and a query supports many groups at once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0067","card":"q-cloudwatch-024-displaying-fields","sec":"cloudwatch-s025","q":"What does this Logs Insights query do, and what are <code>@message</code> and <code>@timestamp</code>?","a":"<p>It shows the raw <code>@message</code> of up to 100 log events. <code>@message</code> is the full text of the log event and <code>@timestamp</code> is the time of the event. CloudWatch adds these automatic fields (also <code>@logStream</code> and <code>@log</code>) to every event.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Plaintext</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>fields @message\n| limit 100</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0068","card":"q-cloudwatch-024-displaying-fields","sec":"cloudwatch-s025","q":"In Logs Insights, how do you extract fields from unstructured log text?","a":"<p>Use <code>parse</code>, for example <code>parse @message \"user=* status=*\" as user, status</code>. It extracts values with a glob or regex into named fields that you can then filter and aggregate. For JSON logs, fields are discovered automatically and can be referenced by name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0069","card":"q-cloudwatch-025-filtering","sec":"cloudwatch-s026","q":"In CloudWatch Logs Insights, what does this query do?","a":"<p>It keeps only log events whose message matches the regular expression <code>exception</code> and shows their message. Commands are chained with the pipe symbol, and each one works on the output of the previous one.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Plaintext</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>fields @message\n| filter @message like /exception/</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0070","card":"q-cloudwatch-025-filtering","sec":"cloudwatch-s026","q":"In Logs Insights, why is <code>like /error/</code> not enough to find every error line, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p><code>like</code> with a regex is case-sensitive, so <code>Error</code> and <code>ERROR</code> are not matched by <code>/error/</code>. Use a case-insensitive pattern such as <code>/(?i)error/</code>, or match the exact levels you log.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0071","card":"q-cloudwatch-025-filtering","sec":"cloudwatch-s026","q":"Why put <code>filter</code> early in a Logs Insights query?","a":"<p>Filtering early shrinks the number of events the later commands process, which makes the query faster. It does not change what is scanned, so cost is controlled mainly by time range and log group choice.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0072","card":"q-cloudwatch-026-aggregating","sec":"cloudwatch-s027","q":"What does this Logs Insights query do, and what does <code>by bin(5m)</code> add?","a":"<p>It counts matching exception events and groups the counts into 5-minute time buckets, giving an error trend over time. Without <code>by bin(5m)</code> you get a single total. <code>bin</code> works on <code>@timestamp</code> and can be shown as a time-series chart.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Plaintext</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>fields @message\n| filter @message like /exception/\n| stats count(*) as error_count by bin(5m)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0073","card":"q-cloudwatch-026-aggregating","sec":"cloudwatch-s027","q":"Which aggregation functions does Logs Insights <code>stats</code> support?","a":"<p><code>count</code>, <code>count_distinct</code>, <code>sum</code>, <code>avg</code>, <code>min</code> and <code>max</code>, plus <code>pct</code> for percentiles. You can group by one or more fields, for example <code>stats count(*) by @logStream</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0074","card":"q-cloudwatch-026-aggregating","sec":"cloudwatch-s027","q":"Logs Insights shows a spike in exception counts. How do you find the cause?","a":"<p>Narrow the time window to the spike, remove the aggregation and list the raw events, expand one to read the full exception and stack trace, then group by a field (such as function, endpoint or error type) to see whether one source dominates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0075","card":"q-cloudwatch-027-command-summary-and-typical-workflow","sec":"cloudwatch-s028","q":"What is the typical workflow for investigating a problem with Logs Insights?","a":"<p>Pick the log group and time window, display the raw events, filter on a keyword or field to isolate the problem, read a full event, then aggregate with <code>stats</code> (optionally by <code>bin</code>) to see frequency and trend. Then turn a recurring query into a metric filter or alarm so you hear about it automatically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0076","card":"q-cloudwatch-027-command-summary-and-typical-workflow","sec":"cloudwatch-s028","q":"What is the order of commands in a typical Logs Insights query, and why does order matter?","a":"<p>Usually <code>fields</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>parse</code>, <code>stats</code>, <code>sort</code>, <code>limit</code>. Each command receives the output of the one before it, so a <code>filter</code> after <code>stats</code> can only use the aggregated fields, and <code>sort</code> before <code>stats</code> is wasted work. Filter first, aggregate next, then order and limit the result.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0077","card":"q-cloudwatch-028-common-query-with-timestamp-and-sort","sec":"cloudwatch-s029","q":"In Logs Insights, what does this query return and why sort by <code>@timestamp desc</code>?","a":"<p>It returns the 100 most recent log events that contain <code>ERROR</code>, newest first, with the time and message. Sorting descending puts the latest failures at the top, and <code>limit</code> is applied after the sort so you get the newest 100 rather than an arbitrary 100.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Plaintext</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>fields @timestamp, @message\n| filter @message like /ERROR/\n| sort @timestamp desc\n| limit 100</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0078","card":"q-cloudwatch-028-common-query-with-timestamp-and-sort","sec":"cloudwatch-s029","q":"What happens in Logs Insights if you put <code>limit 100</code> before <code>sort @timestamp desc</code>?","a":"<p>The query takes 100 events first and sorts only those, so the result is not the 100 newest matches. Put <code>sort</code> before <code>limit</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0079","card":"q-cloudwatch-029-why-convert-logs-to-metrics","sec":"cloudwatch-s030","q":"Why convert log data into a CloudWatch metric?","a":"<p>A metric is cheap to store, fast to graph and can drive an alarm, while querying logs repeatedly is slower and costs per GB scanned. A metric filter counts matching log events as they arrive and publishes a metric, so you can alarm on, for example, validation errors without a manual query.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0080","card":"q-cloudwatch-029-why-convert-logs-to-metrics","sec":"cloudwatch-s030","q":"What is the difference between a metric filter and a Logs Insights query?","a":"<p>A metric filter runs continuously on incoming log events and publishes a metric. It is not retroactive: it only counts events that arrive after it was created. Logs Insights is an on-demand query over stored logs, good for investigation. Use a metric filter for ongoing alarms and trends, and Insights for exploration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0081","card":"q-cloudwatch-029-why-convert-logs-to-metrics","sec":"cloudwatch-s030","q":"How would you get alerted when a specific error appears in your application's logs?","a":"<p>Create a metric filter on the log group that matches the error, with metric value 1 and a custom namespace. Then create an alarm on that metric (for example Sum greater than 0 in 5 minutes) that notifies an SNS topic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0082","card":"q-cloudwatch-030-creating-a-metric-filter","sec":"cloudwatch-s031","q":"What does this CloudWatch Logs metric filter pattern match?","a":"<p>It matches JSON log events where the field <code>errorType</code> equals <code>ValidationException</code>. <code>$.</code> is the root of the JSON event, and braces mark a JSON filter pattern.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Plaintext</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{ $.errorType = &quot;ValidationException&quot; }</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0083","card":"q-cloudwatch-030-creating-a-metric-filter","sec":"cloudwatch-s031","q":"What kinds of filter patterns does CloudWatch Logs support?","a":"<p>Plain terms (for example <code>ERROR</code>), multiple terms (all must appear), exclusion with <code>-term</code>, JSON patterns such as <code>{ $.statusCode = 500 }</code>, and space-delimited patterns for structured text lines such as access logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0084","card":"q-cloudwatch-030-creating-a-metric-filter","sec":"cloudwatch-s031","q":"Why test a metric filter pattern against a real log entry before creating it?","a":"<p>Patterns are exact. A wrong field name, case or quoting gives zero matches, and the metric then stays empty without any error. The Test Pattern tool shows which sample events match, so you find out before you depend on the metric.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0085","card":"q-cloudwatch-031-metric-details","sec":"cloudwatch-s032","q":"In a CloudWatch Logs metric filter, what does the metric value do, and when would you not use 1?","a":"<p>The metric value is what is added to the metric for each matching event. Use <code>1</code> to count occurrences. For JSON logs you can use a field, such as <code>$.duration</code>, to record a value, for example latency or bytes, and then graph its Average or Maximum.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0086","card":"q-cloudwatch-031-metric-details","sec":"cloudwatch-s032","q":"In a CloudWatch Logs metric filter, what is the default value and what changes if you set or omit it?","a":"<p>The default value is what the metric reports for a period when no event matches. If you set <code>0</code>, you get explicit zeros, so graphs are continuous and alarms see data. If you leave it unset, nothing is published when nothing matches, which avoids data points but leaves gaps that alarms treat as missing data (configurable on the alarm).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0087","card":"q-cloudwatch-031-metric-details","sec":"cloudwatch-s032","q":"Why does a CloudWatch alarm on a metric filter's metric sometimes sit in INSUFFICIENT_DATA?","a":"<p>Without a default value the metric has no data points in quiet periods. Set the filter default to <code>0</code>, or set the alarm's missing-data treatment to <code>notBreaching</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0088","card":"q-cloudwatch-031-metric-details","sec":"cloudwatch-s032","q":"What do dimensions on a metric filter let you do, and what is the limitation?","a":"<p>Dimensions let a metric be split by a log field (for example status code or endpoint). For JSON patterns the dimension value can come from a field in the event. The limitation is cardinality: a field with many values creates many metrics and cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0089","card":"q-cloudwatch-032-metric-filter-guidance","sec":"cloudwatch-s033","q":"What are best practices for metric filters in CloudWatch?","a":"<p>Test patterns on real log lines, use meaningful production names (for example a name that says what is counted) and group related metrics in one namespace, then use the metric on dashboards and alarms. Watch the cost, because every filter-created metric is a billed custom metric.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0090","card":"q-cloudwatch-032-metric-filter-guidance","sec":"cloudwatch-s033","q":"How does a metric filter behave conceptually?","a":"<p>Like an <code>if</code> statement run on every incoming log event: if the event matches the pattern, add the metric value to the metric. Events that do not match contribute nothing (or the default value if configured).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0091","card":"q-cloudwatch-032-metric-filter-guidance","sec":"cloudwatch-s033","q":"When would you choose structured JSON logs over plain text for use with metric filters?","a":"<p>JSON lets patterns reference fields directly (<code>$.statusCode = 500</code>), extract numeric values for the metric, and add dimensions from fields. Text patterns rely on matching strings or positional fields and break easily when the format changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0092","card":"q-cloudwatch-033-what-the-agent-does","sec":"cloudwatch-s034","q":"What does the CloudWatch agent do, and why is it needed?","a":"<p>It is software installed on EC2 instances or on-premises servers (Linux and Windows) that collects system-level metrics and logs and sends them to CloudWatch. EC2's default metrics come from the hypervisor, so they do not include memory or disk usage. The agent adds those, plus custom application metrics and log files.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0093","card":"q-cloudwatch-033-what-the-agent-does","sec":"cloudwatch-s034","q":"Which common EC2 metrics are not available in CloudWatch without the agent?","a":"<p>Memory utilization and disk space usage (and in-guest metrics like swap, process counts). Default EC2 metrics cover CPU, network, disk I/O and status checks, measured from outside the instance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0094","card":"q-cloudwatch-033-what-the-agent-does","sec":"cloudwatch-s034","q":"What are StatsD and collectd, and how does the CloudWatch agent use them?","a":"<p>Both are daemons or protocols for collecting application and system metrics. The agent can listen for StatsD messages (over UDP, port 8125 by default) from your app, and read metrics from collectd. StatsD works on Linux and Windows. collectd works on Linux only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0095","card":"q-cloudwatch-033-what-the-agent-does","sec":"cloudwatch-s034","q":"What is the difference between the CloudWatch agent and the older CloudWatch Logs agent?","a":"<p>The unified CloudWatch agent collects both metrics and logs in one package, on Linux and Windows, with a single JSON configuration. The older CloudWatch Logs agent only collected logs and is legacy. New setups should use the unified agent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0096","card":"q-cloudwatch-033-what-the-agent-does","sec":"cloudwatch-s034","q":"When would you use the CloudWatch agent instead of calling <code>PutMetricData</code> from your code?","a":"<p>When you need host-level data (memory, disk, CPU per core), log file collection, or metrics from existing software without changing code. Call <code>PutMetricData</code> (or EMF) from code when the metric is application logic that only your code knows.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0097","card":"q-cloudwatch-034-install-and-configure-outline","sec":"cloudwatch-s035","q":"What are the steps to get the CloudWatch agent working on a server?","a":"<p>Attach an IAM role with agent permissions, install the agent package, create a configuration (with the wizard or by writing the JSON), start the agent with that configuration, then check the metrics under the <code>CWAgent</code> namespace and the log groups.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0098","card":"q-cloudwatch-034-install-and-configure-outline","sec":"cloudwatch-s035","q":"Why store the CloudWatch agent configuration in SSM Parameter Store?","a":"<p>It gives one central copy that many servers can fetch and update, instead of editing a file on every machine. The agent pulls it with the <code>fetch-config</code> action. The trade-off is that the instance role needs SSM read permission, and the Region must match.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0099","card":"q-cloudwatch-034-install-and-configure-outline","sec":"cloudwatch-s035","q":"How can the CloudWatch agent run on an on-premises server that has no instance role?","a":"<p>Create an IAM user (or use IAM Roles Anywhere), place credentials in a shared credentials file for the agent, and start it in on-premises mode (<code>-m onPremise</code>). EC2 mode (<code>-m ec2</code>) is for instances with an instance profile.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0100","card":"q-cloudwatch-035-iam-role-for-the-agent","sec":"cloudwatch-s036","q":"Which IAM policy should a CloudWatch agent instance role use in production, and why not the full-access ones?","a":"<p>Use the AWS managed <code>CloudWatchAgentServerPolicy</code>. It allows only what the agent needs: publishing metrics, writing logs, and reading its configuration from SSM Parameter Store. <code>CloudWatchFullAccess</code> and <code>AmazonEC2FullAccess</code> grant far more than the agent needs and break least privilege.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0101","card":"q-cloudwatch-035-iam-role-for-the-agent","sec":"cloudwatch-s036","q":"What is the difference between <code>CloudWatchAgentServerPolicy</code> and <code>CloudWatchAgentAdminPolicy</code>?","a":"<p><code>CloudWatchAgentServerPolicy</code> is for the instances that run the agent. <code>CloudWatchAgentAdminPolicy</code> additionally allows writing to SSM Parameter Store, so it is for the host where you run the wizard and store the configuration. Do not put the admin policy on every instance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0102","card":"q-cloudwatch-035-iam-role-for-the-agent","sec":"cloudwatch-s036","q":"How do you give an existing EC2 instance the permissions for the CloudWatch agent?","a":"<p>Create an IAM role for EC2 with the needed policy, wrap it in an instance profile, and attach it to the instance (console: Actions, Security, Modify IAM role). The agent then gets temporary credentials from instance metadata. No access keys on the machine.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0103","card":"q-cloudwatch-035-iam-role-for-the-agent","sec":"cloudwatch-s036","q":"The CloudWatch agent is running but no metrics appear in CloudWatch. What do you check?","a":"<p>The instance role and its policies, the agent status, the Region in the configuration, the agent log file for permission or network errors, and network access to the CloudWatch endpoints (internet, NAT or VPC endpoints). Then look under the <code>CWAgent</code> namespace, not the AWS namespaces.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0104","card":"q-cloudwatch-036-linux-setup-install","sec":"cloudwatch-s037","q":"How do you install the CloudWatch agent on Amazon Linux?","a":"<p>With the package manager: <code>sudo yum install amazon-cloudwatch-agent</code> (on Amazon Linux 2023 <code>dnf</code> also works). On other distributions, download the package for your OS from the AWS download URL and install it with <code>rpm</code> or <code>dpkg</code>, or deploy it at scale through Systems Manager Run Command or Distributor.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0105","card":"q-cloudwatch-036-linux-setup-install","sec":"cloudwatch-s037","q":"How would you install and configure the CloudWatch agent on many EC2 instances without logging in to each?","a":"<p>Use AWS Systems Manager. Run the <code>AWS-ConfigureAWSPackage</code> document to install the agent, and the <code>AmazonCloudWatch-ManageAgent</code> document to apply a configuration from Parameter Store. Instances need the SSM agent and an instance role with SSM permissions. You can also bake it into an AMI or user data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0106","card":"q-cloudwatch-037-linux-setup-configuration-wizard","sec":"cloudwatch-s038","q":"Where does the Amazon CloudWatch agent configuration wizard save the JSON file it builds, and what are your options for using it on other servers?","a":"<p>On Linux the wizard writes <code>config.json</code> under <code>/opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/bin/</code>. To reuse it, either store it in SSM Parameter Store (the wizard offers this) and fetch it with <code>-c ssm:&lt;name&gt;</code>, or copy the file and load it with <code>-c file:&lt;path&gt;</code>. SSM is easier for a fleet because every instance reads one central copy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0107","card":"q-cloudwatch-038-linux-setup-collectd-start-and-verify","sec":"cloudwatch-s039","q":"Which CloudWatch namespace do metrics collected by the CloudWatch agent appear in by default, and how can you change it?","a":"<p>The default namespace is <code>CWAgent</code>. You can set a different one with the <code>namespace</code> field in the <code>metrics</code> section of the agent's JSON configuration. Using a separate namespace per application or environment keeps agent metrics easy to find and to scope IAM and alarms.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0108","card":"q-cloudwatch-039-windows-setup-connect-download-install","sec":"cloudwatch-s040","q":"On Windows Server, how is the CloudWatch agent installed, and how does that differ from Amazon Linux?","a":"<p>On Windows you download the <code>amazon-cloudwatch-agent.msi</code> package and install it with <code>msiexec /i</code>. On Amazon Linux you install the <code>amazon-cloudwatch-agent</code> package with <code>yum</code>. Either way the agent then needs an instance role (or credentials) and a configuration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0109","card":"q-cloudwatch-039-windows-setup-connect-download-install","sec":"cloudwatch-s040","q":"How would you install the CloudWatch agent on a fleet of Windows instances without opening an RDP session to each one?","a":"<p>Use AWS Systems Manager Run Command (or State Manager) with the <code>AWS-ConfigureAWSPackage</code> document and the <code>AmazonCloudWatchAgent</code> package. Instances must run the SSM Agent and have an instance profile that allows Systems Manager. You then push the configuration with the <code>AmazonCloudWatch-ManageAgent</code> document.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0110","card":"q-cloudwatch-039-windows-setup-connect-download-install","sec":"cloudwatch-s040","q":"Why does the instance role for the CloudWatch agent on Windows need the same permissions as on Linux?","a":"<p>The agent is the same program on both systems. It calls the same APIs: <code>PutMetricData</code> and CloudWatch Logs actions to send data, EC2 describe calls for instance dimensions, and <code>ssm:GetParameter</code> to read its configuration. The scoped <code>CloudWatchAgentServerPolicy</code> covers these without full-access policies.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0111","card":"q-cloudwatch-040-windows-setup-wizard","sec":"cloudwatch-s041","q":"In the CloudWatch agent on Windows, what can you collect from the Windows Event Log, and which settings control cost?","a":"<p>The agent can send Windows Event Log channels (such as System, Application, Security) to a CloudWatch Logs group. You choose the event levels (Information, Warning, Error, Critical) and the format (XML or text). Cost is driven by volume: ingestion is billed per GB, so in production collect only Warning and more severe, and only the channels you use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0112","card":"q-cloudwatch-040-windows-setup-wizard","sec":"cloudwatch-s041","q":"Why does a CloudWatch agent configuration for Windows use performance counters instead of the Linux-style metrics?","a":"<p>Windows exposes host data through performance counters (processor, memory, logical disk, network). The agent reads those and publishes them to the <code>CWAgent</code> namespace. Linux has no such counters, so the agent uses <code>/proc</code>-based collection there. The wizard offers the right set for the detected OS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0113","card":"q-cloudwatch-040-windows-setup-wizard","sec":"cloudwatch-s041","q":"Why is the CloudWatch agent's per-core CPU option something to enable carefully on hosts with many cores?","a":"<p>Each core becomes its own metric (one per dimension combination), and each custom metric is billed monthly. A 64-core host can add 64 CPU metrics per instance. Use totals unless you need to see single-core saturation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0114","card":"q-cloudwatch-041-windows-setup-start-and-verify","sec":"cloudwatch-s042","q":"How do you verify that the CloudWatch agent is running on Windows, and where do you read its log?","a":"<p>Check the service with <code>Get-Service -Name AmazonCloudWatchAgent</code>; its status should be <code>Running</code>. The agent log is at <code>C:\\ProgramData\\Amazon\\AmazonCloudWatchAgent\\Logs\\amazon-cloudwatch-agent.log</code>. It shows IAM, parameter and network errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0115","card":"q-cloudwatch-041-windows-setup-start-and-verify","sec":"cloudwatch-s042","q":"On Windows, the CloudWatch agent control script fails to fetch <code>ssm:AmazonCloudWatch-windows</code>. What do you check?","a":"<p>First confirm the parameter exists in Systems Manager Parameter Store in the same region the agent uses. Then confirm the instance role allows <code>ssm:GetParameter</code>. Also check the exact parameter name and the <code>-m ec2</code> or <code>-m onPremise</code> mode. Errors appear in the agent log.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0116","card":"q-cloudwatch-041-windows-setup-start-and-verify","sec":"cloudwatch-s042","q":"Can the CloudWatch agent use collectd on Windows?","a":"<p>No. collectd is supported only on Linux. On Windows use StatsD for application metrics and performance counters for host metrics.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0117","card":"q-cloudwatch-042-what-it-is","sec":"cloudwatch-s043","q":"What is metric math in CloudWatch, and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>Metric math lets you compute new time series from existing CloudWatch metrics using expressions. You can combine metrics (for example errors divided by invocations) without publishing a new custom metric. Results can be graphed, added to dashboards and used in alarms.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0118","card":"q-cloudwatch-042-what-it-is","sec":"cloudwatch-s043","q":"In CloudWatch metric math, what are the IDs on metrics and expressions for?","a":"<p>Each metric gets an ID such as <code>m1</code> and each expression gets one such as <code>e1</code>. Expressions refer to those IDs, so one expression can build on another. IDs must start with a lowercase letter in the API and in alarm configuration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0119","card":"q-cloudwatch-042-what-it-is","sec":"cloudwatch-s043","q":"When would you use metric math instead of publishing a custom metric?","a":"<p>Use metric math when the value can be derived from metrics you already have: ratios, sums across resources, rates of change. It costs nothing in storage and is always up to date. Publish a custom metric when the value only exists inside your application, or when you need it kept beyond what the source metrics provide.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0120","card":"q-cloudwatch-043-built-in-functions","sec":"cloudwatch-s044","q":"In CloudWatch metric math, what does <code>RATE</code> return and what does it compute?","a":"<p><code>RATE(m1)</code> returns the rate of change of the metric per second: the difference between the latest and the previous data point divided by the seconds between them. It suits counters that only grow. On sparse data, alarms on <code>RATE</code> can behave unpredictably.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0121","card":"q-cloudwatch-043-built-in-functions","sec":"cloudwatch-s044","q":"In CloudWatch metric math, what does <code>FILL</code> do, and what filler values can it use?","a":"<p><code>FILL</code> replaces missing data points. The filler can be a number such as <code>FILL(m1, 0)</code>, another metric, <code>REPEAT</code> (the last real value) or <code>LINEAR</code> (interpolation across the gap). Use 0 for count metrics where no data means nothing happened. Use <code>REPEAT</code> for gauges.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0122","card":"q-cloudwatch-043-built-in-functions","sec":"cloudwatch-s044","q":"In CloudWatch metric math, what does the <code>DAY</code> function return and how can you use it?","a":"<p><code>DAY</code> returns the UTC day of the week for each timestamp as an integer from 1 to 7. Combined with <code>IF</code>, it filters data by weekday, for example keeping only weekends. Because it is UTC, results shift for users in other time zones.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0123","card":"q-cloudwatch-043-built-in-functions","sec":"cloudwatch-s044","q":"In CloudWatch metric math, what does <code>IF</code> do, and what is its general form?","a":"<p><code>IF</code> filters or mixes time series using a comparison: <code>IF(condition, valueIfTrue, valueIfFalse)</code>. For example <code>IF(m1 &gt; 100, m1, 0)</code> keeps values over 100 and replaces the rest with 0. The function name must be upper case.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0124","card":"q-cloudwatch-044-practical-tips","sec":"cloudwatch-s045","q":"In CloudWatch metric math, what does <code>METRICS()</code> return and why can it mislead you?","a":"<p><code>METRICS()</code> returns every metric in the graph (not other expressions). In an expression such as <code>AVG(METRICS())</code> it aggregates all metrics you selected, which is often not what you meant when the graph holds unrelated metrics. Use specific IDs like <code>m1</code>, or <code>METRICS(\"errors\")</code> to match IDs containing a string.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0125","card":"q-cloudwatch-044-practical-tips","sec":"cloudwatch-s045","q":"A CloudWatch graph shows no data after you add a math expression. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check that the time range covers data (sparse metrics need a wider window), that the metric statistics are the ones the expression needs (for example Sum instead of Average), that the periods of the metrics match, and that the expression refers to existing IDs. Also check that all metrics are in the same region unless you use cross-region metric math.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0126","card":"q-cloudwatch-044-practical-tips","sec":"cloudwatch-s045","q":"What limits apply to the number of metrics and expressions on one CloudWatch graph?","a":"<p>A graph supports up to 500 metrics and expressions together. This applies even to metrics that are hidden. Beyond that, split the graph or use search expressions to reduce what is shown.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0127","card":"q-cloudwatch-045-custom-expressions","sec":"cloudwatch-s046","q":"In CloudWatch metric math, how would you compute the average duration per invocation of a Lambda function?","a":"<p>Divide the <code>Duration</code> metric (as a Sum) by the <code>Invocations</code> metric (as a Sum): <code>m1 / m2</code>. The result is the mean duration in milliseconds per call. Using the <code>Average</code> statistic on <code>Duration</code> directly gives the same figure; the division is useful when you combine different metrics.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0128","card":"q-cloudwatch-045-custom-expressions","sec":"cloudwatch-s046","q":"What is the metric math expression for a Lambda error rate percentage, and what is its pitfall?","a":"<p>Here <code>m1</code> is <code>Errors</code> (Sum) and <code>m2</code> is <code>Invocations</code> (Sum). The result is the percent of calls that failed. The pitfall is a period with zero invocations: the division has no data, so the result is missing rather than 0. Use <code>FILL</code> or alarm settings for missing data to handle it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Plaintext</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>(m1 / m2) * 100</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0129","card":"q-cloudwatch-045-custom-expressions","sec":"cloudwatch-s046","q":"In CloudWatch metric math, what does this expression do, where <code>e1</code> is an error-rate expression?","a":"<p>It keeps the values of <code>e1</code> that are 25 or higher and replaces all others with 0. This highlights only the periods where an error-rate series exceeds a threshold, which is useful for graphs and for building alarms on a filtered series.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Plaintext</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>IF(e1 &gt;= 25, e1, 0)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0130","card":"q-cloudwatch-045-custom-expressions","sec":"cloudwatch-s046","q":"Can you create a CloudWatch alarm on a metric math expression, and what is the benefit?","a":"<p>Yes. You can alarm on an expression such as an error percentage. The benefit is that the threshold stays meaningful as traffic changes, and you avoid publishing a custom metric. Alarms cannot use <code>SEARCH</code> expressions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0131","card":"q-cloudwatch-046-why-dashboards","sec":"cloudwatch-s047","q":"Why use CloudWatch dashboards instead of ad hoc graphs in the metrics console?","a":"<p>Console graphs are not saved. A dashboard stores the widgets, so the team sees the same view each time. It gives a single overview for operations and can be shared.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0132","card":"q-cloudwatch-046-why-dashboards","sec":"cloudwatch-s047","q":"What can a CloudWatch dashboard show besides metric line graphs?","a":"<p>Widgets include line, stacked area, number, gauge, bar and pie graphs for metrics, text, alarm status, Logs Insights query results, and explorer widgets. Mixing metrics, alarm states and log queries on one page helps during incidents.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0133","card":"q-cloudwatch-046-why-dashboards","sec":"cloudwatch-s047","q":"Can a CloudWatch dashboard show data from several regions or accounts?","a":"<p>Yes. Dashboards are global and widgets can point to metrics in different regions. With cross-account observability or cross-account dashboards set up, widgets can also show other accounts' data. Access is controlled by IAM.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0134","card":"q-cloudwatch-046-why-dashboards","sec":"cloudwatch-s047","q":"How would you manage CloudWatch dashboards as code?","a":"<p>A dashboard is a JSON body of widgets. You can create it with <code>put_dashboard</code> in Boto3, the CLI, CloudFormation (<code>AWS::CloudWatch::Dashboard</code>) or Terraform. Keeping the JSON in version control makes dashboards reviewable and repeatable across environments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0135","card":"q-cloudwatch-047-adding-a-graph-to-a-dashboard","sec":"cloudwatch-s048","q":"In the CloudWatch console, what are the two ways to put a graph on a dashboard?","a":"<p>Either create a widget on the dashboard and define the metrics (and math) inside it, or build the graph in the metrics view and use Actions, then Add to dashboard. The second is quicker when you have already tuned the graph. In both cases save the dashboard, or the changes are lost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0136","card":"q-cloudwatch-047-adding-a-graph-to-a-dashboard","sec":"cloudwatch-s048","q":"Why do you choose a line widget for latency and a number widget for the current error count on a CloudWatch dashboard?","a":"<p>A line shows the trend over time, which is what you need to spot a latency regression. A number widget shows one current value, which suits a quick status such as the latest error count. Match the widget to the question the reader asks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0137","card":"q-cloudwatch-048-what-an-alarm-does","sec":"cloudwatch-s049","q":"What does a CloudWatch alarm do, and what can it trigger?","a":"<p>An alarm watches one metric (or a metric math expression) against a threshold over a number of periods. When its state changes, it can send an SNS notification, run an Auto Scaling policy, run EC2 actions such as stop, terminate, reboot or recover, or create a Systems Manager OpsItem or incident.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0138","card":"q-cloudwatch-048-what-an-alarm-does","sec":"cloudwatch-s049","q":"Why use a CloudWatch alarm instead of checking dashboards manually?","a":"<p>Alarms watch continuously and notify or act on their own. People cannot watch graphs around the clock, and automated actions (such as scaling out) can respond faster than a human. The alarm state history also gives an audit trail.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0139","card":"q-cloudwatch-048-what-an-alarm-does","sec":"cloudwatch-s049","q":"What is the difference between a CloudWatch alarm and an EventBridge rule?","a":"<p>An alarm evaluates a metric against a threshold over time. An EventBridge rule matches discrete events, such as a state change or an API call. Use alarms for \"CPU is high for 10 minutes\" and EventBridge for \"an instance was terminated\". You can also trigger EventBridge rules from alarm state changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0140","card":"q-cloudwatch-048-what-an-alarm-does","sec":"cloudwatch-s049","q":"What is a CloudWatch composite alarm, and why use one?","a":"<p>A composite alarm combines other alarms with AND, OR and NOT rules. It alarms only when the combination is true, such as high CPU and high latency. This reduces noise because you page on one meaningful condition, not on each metric alarm.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0141","card":"q-cloudwatch-049-alarm-states","sec":"cloudwatch-s050","q":"What are the three states of a CloudWatch metric alarm and what does each mean?","a":"<p><code>OK</code> means the metric is within the threshold. <code>ALARM</code> means the threshold was breached for the required number of periods. <code>INSUFFICIENT_DATA</code> means the alarm has just started, the metric is not available, or not enough data points have arrived to decide.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0142","card":"q-cloudwatch-049-alarm-states","sec":"cloudwatch-s050","q":"A CloudWatch alarm sits in <code>INSUFFICIENT_DATA</code> and never goes to <code>OK</code>. What can cause that?","a":"<p>The metric has no data points. Common causes are a wrong namespace, metric name or dimension value, a different region, a metric that is only published during activity (for example Lambda <code>Errors</code> with no traffic), or a period shorter than the metric's publishing interval. Check the metric in the console with the same dimensions, and set the missing-data option if sparse data is expected.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0143","card":"q-cloudwatch-049-alarm-states","sec":"cloudwatch-s050","q":"In CloudWatch alarms, what does the \"treat missing data\" setting do and what are the choices?","a":"<p>It sets how the alarm evaluates periods with no data points. The choices are <code>missing</code> (the default; the alarm keeps its state or moves to insufficient data), <code>notBreaching</code> (counts as within threshold), <code>breaching</code> (counts as a breach) and <code>ignore</code> (keeps the current state). Use <code>breaching</code> for a heartbeat metric whose silence means failure, and <code>notBreaching</code> for count metrics where no data means no errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0144","card":"q-cloudwatch-049-alarm-states","sec":"cloudwatch-s050","q":"In CloudWatch alarms, what do <code>EvaluationPeriods</code> and <code>DatapointsToAlarm</code> mean and when would you set both?","a":"<p><code>EvaluationPeriods</code> is how many of the latest periods are checked. <code>DatapointsToAlarm</code> is how many of those must breach to go to <code>ALARM</code> (\"M out of N\"). With 3 out of 5, a short spike does not alarm but sustained trouble does. This reduces false alarms without waiting for N consecutive breaches.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0145","card":"q-cloudwatch-049-alarm-states","sec":"cloudwatch-s050","q":"Why configure a CloudWatch alarm to send a notification for the <code>OK</code> state too?","a":"<p>The <code>OK</code> notification tells the team the problem is resolved, so people stop investigating and do not duplicate work. It also confirms that an automatic recovery worked.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0146","card":"q-cloudwatch-050-alarm-on-lambda-errors-console-steps","sec":"cloudwatch-s051","q":"When would you choose a static threshold and when anomaly detection for a CloudWatch alarm?","a":"<p>Use a static threshold when you know the limit, such as an error count or CPU over 80%. Use anomaly detection when normal values change with time, such as daily or weekly traffic cycles, because a band learned from history adapts to the pattern. Anomaly detection needs a training period and suits metrics with stable patterns.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0147","card":"q-cloudwatch-050-alarm-on-lambda-errors-console-steps","sec":"cloudwatch-s051","q":"You subscribed an email address to the SNS topic used by a CloudWatch alarm, but no email arrives when the alarm fires. What do you check?","a":"<p>First check that the subscriber confirmed the subscription from the confirmation email. An unconfirmed subscription gets nothing. Then check the spam folder, whether the alarm actually has actions enabled for that state (<code>ActionsEnabled</code>, and the topic ARN in the correct alarm or OK action list), the topic's access policy, and the alarm's history for failed actions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0148","card":"q-cloudwatch-050-alarm-on-lambda-errors-console-steps","sec":"cloudwatch-s051","q":"How can you test that a CloudWatch alarm and its notification work without causing a real incident?","a":"<p>Use the <code>SetAlarmState</code> API (<code>aws cloudwatch set-alarm-state</code>) to move the alarm to <code>ALARM</code> temporarily, which fires its actions. The alarm returns to its real state at the next evaluation. This tests the notification path. To test the metric and threshold too, generate real failing traffic in a non-production environment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0149","card":"q-cloudwatch-050-alarm-on-lambda-errors-console-steps","sec":"cloudwatch-s051","q":"Why does a short period such as 1 minute make a CloudWatch alarm react faster, and what is the trade-off?","a":"<p>The alarm evaluates after each period, so a 1-minute period detects trouble in about a minute. The trade-offs are more noise from short blips and, for some services, no 1-minute data unless detailed monitoring is on.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0150","card":"q-cloudwatch-051-alarm-on-lambda-errors-boto3","sec":"cloudwatch-s052","q":"In Boto3, how does <code>put_metric_alarm</code> define an alarm on Lambda errors and which fields identify the metric?","a":"<p>The metric is identified by <code>Namespace</code> (<code>AWS/Lambda</code>), <code>MetricName</code> (<code>Errors</code>) and <code>Dimensions</code> (the function name). The other fields define the rule: one 60-second period with a Sum over 20 puts the alarm in <code>ALARM</code>. If the dimension is wrong, the alarm watches a metric that has no data and stays in <code>INSUFFICIENT_DATA</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>cloudwatch.put_metric_alarm(\n    AlarmName=&#x27;apiadmins&#x27;,\n    ComparisonOperator=&#x27;GreaterThanThreshold&#x27;,\n    EvaluationPeriods=1,\n    MetricName=&#x27;Errors&#x27;,\n    Namespace=&#x27;AWS/Lambda&#x27;,\n    Period=60,\n    Statistic=&#x27;Sum&#x27;,\n    Threshold=20.0,\n    ...\n    Dimensions=[{&#x27;Name&#x27;: &#x27;FunctionName&#x27;, &#x27;Value&#x27;: &#x27;putitemFunction&#x27;}]\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0151","card":"q-cloudwatch-051-alarm-on-lambda-errors-boto3","sec":"cloudwatch-s052","q":"In Boto3, what does <code>put_metric_alarm</code> do if an alarm with the same <code>AlarmName</code> already exists?","a":"<p>It updates the existing alarm with the new settings instead of failing. This makes the call idempotent and suitable for scripts and infrastructure automation. Be careful that two scripts using one name overwrite each other.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0152","card":"q-cloudwatch-051-alarm-on-lambda-errors-boto3","sec":"cloudwatch-s052","q":"In Boto3 <code>put_metric_alarm</code>, what is the difference between <code>AlarmActions</code>, <code>OKActions</code> and <code>InsufficientDataActions</code>?","a":"<p>Each is a list of action ARNs run when the alarm enters that state: <code>ALARM</code>, <code>OK</code> or <code>INSUFFICIENT_DATA</code>. Actions run only on a change of state, not at every evaluation. They take effect only if <code>ActionsEnabled</code> is <code>True</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0153","card":"q-cloudwatch-051-alarm-on-lambda-errors-boto3","sec":"cloudwatch-s052","q":"In Boto3 <code>put_metric_alarm</code>, how do you alarm on a percentile such as p99 latency?","a":"<p>Use <code>ExtendedStatistic='p99'</code> and leave out <code>Statistic</code>. <code>Statistic</code> accepts only Sum, Average, Minimum, Maximum and SampleCount. Percentile alarms suit latency, where averages hide slow outliers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0154","card":"q-cloudwatch-051-alarm-on-lambda-errors-boto3","sec":"cloudwatch-s052","q":"In Boto3 <code>put_metric_alarm</code>, how would you alarm on an error rate instead of a raw error count?","a":"<p>Pass a <code>Metrics</code> list with metric queries and an expression, for example <code>Errors</code> and <code>Invocations</code> as two queries and <code>(m1/m2)*100</code> with <code>ReturnData=True</code>, and use <code>Threshold</code> on that result instead of <code>MetricName</code>. The threshold then holds for any traffic level.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0155","card":"q-cloudwatch-052-alarm-that-notifies-through-sns-on-high","sec":"cloudwatch-s053","q":"What does this alarm configuration mean in terms of time, and why use two evaluation periods?","a":"<p>The alarm fires when average CPU is over 70% in two consecutive 5-minute periods, so after about 10 minutes of sustained load. Two periods filter out short spikes, such as a batch job. The cost is slower detection.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>...\n    EvaluationPeriods=2,\n    MetricName=&#x27;CPUUtilization&#x27;,\n    Namespace=&#x27;AWS/EC2&#x27;,\n    Period=300,\n    Statistic=&#x27;Average&#x27;,\n    Threshold=70.0,</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0156","card":"q-cloudwatch-052-alarm-that-notifies-through-sns-on-high","sec":"cloudwatch-s053","q":"Why does an EC2 CPU alarm with a 1-minute period need detailed monitoring?","a":"<p>Basic monitoring publishes EC2 metrics every 5 minutes, so a 60-second period would see data only every fifth period. Detailed monitoring publishes every minute, at an extra charge. Match the alarm's period to the metric's publishing interval.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0157","card":"q-cloudwatch-052-alarm-that-notifies-through-sns-on-high","sec":"cloudwatch-s053","q":"In CloudWatch, how do you alarm on CPU of one EC2 instance, and how for a whole Auto Scaling group?","a":"<p>For one instance, use the <code>CPUUtilization</code> metric with the <code>InstanceId</code> dimension. For the group, use the <code>AutoScalingGroupName</code> dimension, which aggregates the instances in the group. Use the group dimension for scaling decisions so the alarm reflects the fleet average.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0158","card":"q-cloudwatch-052-alarm-that-notifies-through-sns-on-high","sec":"cloudwatch-s053","q":"What is the role of SNS in a CloudWatch alarm notification?","a":"<p>SNS is the delivery layer. The alarm publishes a message to a topic and SNS fans it out to subscribers: email, SMS, HTTPS endpoints, Lambda, SQS or chat integrations. Separating the two means you can add or change recipients without touching the alarm.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0159","card":"q-cloudwatch-053-alarm-actions-with-auto-scaling-create-t","sec":"cloudwatch-s054","q":"In an EC2 Auto Scaling group, why must the maximum capacity be higher than the desired capacity for scale-out to work?","a":"<p>Scaling out raises the desired capacity, but never past the maximum. If the maximum equals the current desired capacity, a scale-out policy runs but adds no instance. Set the maximum with headroom for peak load and cost limits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0160","card":"q-cloudwatch-053-alarm-actions-with-auto-scaling-create-t","sec":"cloudwatch-s054","q":"What is a launch template in EC2 Auto Scaling and why use it?","a":"<p>A launch template defines how instances are created: AMI, instance type, key pair, security groups, IAM instance profile and user data. The group uses it for each new instance, so they are identical. Launch templates are versioned and are the recommended replacement for launch configurations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0161","card":"q-cloudwatch-053-alarm-actions-with-auto-scaling-create-t","sec":"cloudwatch-s054","q":"What do minimum, desired and maximum capacity mean in an Auto Scaling group?","a":"<p>Minimum is the fewest instances the group keeps. Maximum is the most it can ever have. Desired is the number it tries to run now; scaling policies and manual changes move the desired value between the minimum and the maximum.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0162","card":"q-cloudwatch-054-alarm-actions-with-auto-scaling-policies","sec":"cloudwatch-s055","q":"What is the cooldown in an Auto Scaling simple scaling policy, and why is it needed?","a":"<p>The cooldown is the time (300 seconds by default) the group waits after a scaling activity before another simple-scaling activity can start. It gives new instances time to start and take load. Without it, the alarm could trigger repeated scale-outs before the first instance is ready, which wastes money.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0163","card":"q-cloudwatch-054-alarm-actions-with-auto-scaling-policies","sec":"cloudwatch-s055","q":"What is the difference between simple, step and target tracking scaling policies in EC2 Auto Scaling?","a":"<p>Simple scaling runs one adjustment per alarm breach and then waits for the cooldown. Step scaling uses several adjustments based on how far the metric is beyond the threshold, and responds to further breaches during an activity. Target tracking keeps a metric near a target value (for example 50% CPU) and creates and manages the alarms for you. AWS recommends target tracking for most cases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0164","card":"q-cloudwatch-054-alarm-actions-with-auto-scaling-policies","sec":"cloudwatch-s055","q":"In this alarm, the <code>OKActions</code> entry runs a scale-in policy. What problem can this design cause?","a":"<p><code>OK</code> means CPU is at or under 70%, not low. The group would scale in as soon as CPU drops to 69%, which can push it back over 70% and cause flapping. Use a separate alarm with a lower threshold (such as 30%) for scale-in, or use target tracking.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>...\n    Threshold=70.0,\n    AlarmActions=[&#x27;arn:aws:autoscaling:us-east-1:123456789012:scalingPolicy:policy-id:scale-up&#x27;],\n    OKActions=[&#x27;arn:aws:autoscaling:us-east-1:123456789012:scalingPolicy:policy-id:scale-down&#x27;],</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0165","card":"q-cloudwatch-054-alarm-actions-with-auto-scaling-policies","sec":"cloudwatch-s055","q":"In a CloudWatch alarm for Auto Scaling, what does the scaling policy ARN in <code>AlarmActions</code> represent?","a":"<p>It identifies the scaling policy of the group to run when the alarm enters <code>ALARM</code>. You get the ARN from the policy when you create it (for example with <code>put_scaling_policy</code>). Alarm and policy must be in the same region.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0166","card":"q-cloudwatch-054-alarm-actions-with-auto-scaling-policies","sec":"cloudwatch-s055","q":"Why do CloudWatch alarms for scaling an Auto Scaling group use the <code>AutoScalingGroupName</code> dimension?","a":"<p>It makes the metric the average (or other statistic) across all instances in the group, so the alarm reflects fleet load and not one instance. A single instance's spike should not scale the whole group.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0167","card":"q-cloudwatch-055-alarm-actions-with-auto-scaling-test-wit","sec":"cloudwatch-s056","q":"What does <code>stress-ng --cpu 4 --timeout 60s</code> do, and why is it used when testing a CPU alarm?","a":"<p><code>stress-ng</code> is a Linux load generator. <code>--cpu 4</code> starts four CPU worker processes and <code>--timeout 60s</code> stops them after 60 seconds. It pushes CPU past the alarm threshold on demand, so you can see the alarm and scaling policy work. Without enough workers for the instance's vCPU count, CPU may not cross the threshold.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0168","card":"q-cloudwatch-055-alarm-actions-with-auto-scaling-test-wit","sec":"cloudwatch-s056","q":"You load-tested an Auto Scaling group, the alarm went to <code>ALARM</code>, but no instance was added. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check that maximum capacity allows another instance, that the policy is attached to the alarm action and the ARN is correct, that the group is not in a cooldown or warmup, that <code>ActionsEnabled</code> is true, and the group's scaling activity history for errors such as a launch failure from quota or subnet capacity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0169","card":"q-cloudwatch-055-alarm-actions-with-auto-scaling-test-wit","sec":"cloudwatch-s056","q":"Why is it better to test an alarm-driven scaling setup with a synthetic load than to wait for real traffic?","a":"<p>You can confirm the whole chain (metric, threshold, alarm, scaling policy, new instance in service, scale-in) before an incident. You also see real timings such as detection delay and instance warmup, which tell you whether thresholds and cooldowns are right.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0170","card":"q-cloudwatch-056-alarm-on-rum-javascript-errors","sec":"cloudwatch-s057","q":"What is CloudWatch RUM and how does it differ from synthetic monitoring?","a":"<p>RUM (Real User Monitoring) collects performance and error data from real users' browsers through a small JavaScript snippet. Synthetic monitoring runs scripted checks on a schedule. RUM shows what users actually experience, but only when users visit. Canaries find problems when nobody is using the site.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0171","card":"q-cloudwatch-056-alarm-on-rum-javascript-errors","sec":"cloudwatch-s057","q":"What does an alarm on the <code>JsErrorCount</code> metric in the <code>AWS/RUM</code> namespace tell you?","a":"<p>It tells you how many JavaScript errors users' browsers reported in the period. A Sum over a threshold means a front-end release or a third-party script is failing for users, even when the backend is healthy. Filter by application monitor name to scope it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0172","card":"q-cloudwatch-056-alarm-on-rum-javascript-errors","sec":"cloudwatch-s057","q":"A RUM-based CloudWatch alarm stays in <code>INSUFFICIENT_DATA</code> during testing. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check that the app monitor's snippet is loaded and that requests reach the RUM endpoint (browser developer tools, Network tab). Check the sampling rate of sessions, since only a share of sessions are recorded, and that the alarm's dimensions match the app monitor name. Allow a couple of minutes for data to arrive.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0173","card":"q-cloudwatch-057-what-synthetics-does","sec":"cloudwatch-s058","q":"What is a CloudWatch Synthetics canary, and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>A canary is a script that runs on a schedule to act like a user or client against your endpoints or website. It finds outages and slow responses before customers report them, including at night when traffic is low. It runs as a Lambda function managed by CloudWatch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0174","card":"q-cloudwatch-057-what-synthetics-does","sec":"cloudwatch-s058","q":"What runtimes can CloudWatch Synthetics canaries use?","a":"<p>Canaries are written in Node.js (with Puppeteer for browser steps) or Python (with Selenium). Which runtime versions are available changes over time, so choose a supported version from the console list. API canaries can also use plain HTTP requests without a browser.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0175","card":"q-cloudwatch-057-what-synthetics-does","sec":"cloudwatch-s058","q":"Where does CloudWatch Synthetics store a canary's screenshots, logs and HAR files?","a":"<p>In an Amazon S3 bucket that you choose (or one created for you). The canary's IAM role needs permission to write there. Logs also go to CloudWatch Logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0176","card":"q-cloudwatch-057-what-synthetics-does","sec":"cloudwatch-s058","q":"Why would you use a canary instead of a Route 53 health check?","a":"<p>A Route 53 health check does simple probes of an endpoint: TCP, HTTP or string match. A canary can run multi-step flows (log in, call an API, validate a response body), take screenshots and report step-level timing. Use health checks for DNS failover and canaries for deeper functional checks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0177","card":"q-cloudwatch-057-what-synthetics-does","sec":"cloudwatch-s058","q":"How often can a CloudWatch Synthetics canary run?","a":"<p>As often as once per minute, on a <code>rate()</code> or <code>cron()</code> schedule, or once on demand. Each run is billed, so the more frequent the schedule and the more canaries you have, the more it costs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0178","card":"q-cloudwatch-058-basic-api-health-canary-script","sec":"cloudwatch-s059","q":"In a CloudWatch Synthetics Node.js canary, what does <code>executeHttpStep</code> do and why use it instead of a raw HTTP call?","a":"<p><code>executeHttpStep</code> makes an HTTP request and records it as a named step. Synthetics reports its duration, status and success as step metrics, and logs the request and response details. A raw HTTP library call would work but would not show up as a step in canary results.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>let response = await synthetics.executeHttpStep(&#x27;Check API Health&#x27;, requestOptions);</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0179","card":"q-cloudwatch-058-basic-api-health-canary-script","sec":"cloudwatch-s059","q":"In a CloudWatch Synthetics canary, why does the script throw an error when the status code is not 200?","a":"<p>A canary run passes only if the handler finishes without an exception. Throwing marks the run as failed, which lowers <code>SuccessPercent</code> and lets an alarm fire. If the script just logged the bad status, the run would show as passed.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>if (response.statusCode !== 200) {\n    throw new Error(&quot;API health check failed!&quot;);\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0180","card":"q-cloudwatch-058-basic-api-health-canary-script","sec":"cloudwatch-s059","q":"What should a good API health-check endpoint test, and what is a weakness of a check that only returns 200 from a static route?","a":"<p>A good health check exercises the dependencies that matter (database, cache, downstream service) and returns an error when they fail. A static route that always returns 200 shows only that the web server is up, so the canary stays green while the app is broken.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0181","card":"q-cloudwatch-058-basic-api-health-canary-script","sec":"cloudwatch-s059","q":"Why use <code>async</code>/<code>await</code> with <code>executeHttpStep</code> in a Synthetics canary handler?","a":"<p>The call returns a promise. Awaiting it makes the script wait for the response before checking the status code or starting the next step. Without <code>await</code>, the handler can finish before the request completes, and the canary reports the wrong result.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0182","card":"q-cloudwatch-059-alarm-on-canary-success-rate","sec":"cloudwatch-s060","q":"In CloudWatch, which canary metric would you alarm on to detect that an endpoint is failing, and under which namespace?","a":"<p>Alarm on <code>SuccessPercent</code>, published in the <code>CloudWatchSynthetics</code> namespace with the <code>CanaryName</code> dimension. It is the percent of runs that passed in the period. Use the <code>Average</code> statistic and the <code>LessThanThreshold</code> operator. The <code>Duration</code> metric covers latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0183","card":"q-cloudwatch-059-alarm-on-canary-success-rate","sec":"cloudwatch-s060","q":"Why is <code>SuccessPercent</code> with <code>LessThanThreshold</code> and a threshold of 90 a reasonable canary alarm?","a":"<p>Average <code>SuccessPercent</code> falls under 90 when more than 10% of runs in the period fail. This allows an occasional transient failure but catches real outages. With one run per minute and a 60-second period, a single failed run drops the value to 0 and breaches, so use more evaluation periods or a coarser period to avoid noise.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>--metric-name SuccessPercent \\\n--threshold 90.0 \\\n--comparison-operator LessThanThreshold \\\n--evaluation-periods 1 \\\n--period 60 \\\n--statistic Average</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0184","card":"q-cloudwatch-059-alarm-on-canary-success-rate","sec":"cloudwatch-s060","q":"If a canary stops running, how should its CloudWatch alarm treat missing data, and why?","a":"<p>Set missing data to <code>breaching</code>. When the canary is deleted, throttled or misconfigured, it publishes nothing, and the default treatment would keep the alarm in <code>OK</code> or <code>INSUFFICIENT_DATA</code> while the endpoint is unmonitored. Treating silence as a breach makes a broken canary visible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0185","card":"q-cloudwatch-059-alarm-on-canary-success-rate","sec":"cloudwatch-s060","q":"How do you use <code>aws cloudwatch put-metric-alarm</code> to notify a team when a canary fails?","a":"<p>Set <code>--alarm-actions</code> to the ARN of an SNS topic with the team's subscribers. Give the alarm the canary metric (<code>SuccessPercent</code>), the <code>CanaryName</code> dimension and a threshold. Add <code>--ok-actions</code> to notify on recovery.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0186","card":"q-cloudwatch-060-creating-a-heartbeat-canary","sec":"cloudwatch-s061","q":"What are the ways to create a CloudWatch Synthetics canary, and when would you use each?","a":"<p>Use a blueprint (heartbeat monitoring, API canary, broken link checker, GUI workflow, visual monitoring) for a quick start. Use the inline editor to write or change a script by hand. Import from S3 to reuse a script maintained in source control. For production, scripts in version control and deployed by automation are easier to review.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0187","card":"q-cloudwatch-060-creating-a-heartbeat-canary","sec":"cloudwatch-s061","q":"What does a CloudWatch Synthetics heartbeat canary check?","a":"<p>It loads a URL in a headless browser, takes a screenshot and confirms the page loads successfully. It tells you that the site is reachable and renders, but not that business functions work. Use API or workflow canaries for those.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0188","card":"q-cloudwatch-060-creating-a-heartbeat-canary","sec":"cloudwatch-s061","q":"In CloudWatch Synthetics, why enable screenshots on a canary, and what is a drawback?","a":"<p>Screenshots show what the page looked like on a failed run, which speeds up debugging. The drawbacks are S3 storage and the chance that screenshots contain sensitive user or test data. Keep a retention rule on the bucket.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0189","card":"q-cloudwatch-060-creating-a-heartbeat-canary","sec":"cloudwatch-s061","q":"How would you set the schedule of a CloudWatch Synthetics canary, and which options exist?","a":"<p>Choose a rate (such as every 5 minutes), a <code>cron</code> expression, or a single run. Pick the frequency from how fast you must detect an outage, balanced against cost and load on the endpoint. Avoid running many canaries at the same second against one target.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0190","card":"q-cloudwatch-061-reading-canary-results","sec":"cloudwatch-s062","q":"What is a HAR file in a CloudWatch Synthetics canary run and how do you use it?","a":"<p>A HAR (HTTP Archive) file records every network request the browser made during the run: URL, method, status, size and timing. Read it to find slow or failing requests, such as a large image or a slow third-party script, in the same way as the Network tab of browser developer tools.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0191","card":"q-cloudwatch-061-reading-canary-results","sec":"cloudwatch-s062","q":"A CloudWatch Synthetics canary run failed. What artifacts do you look at to find the cause?","a":"<p>Open the run's steps and see which step failed, then check the screenshot of that step, the canary's logs (the error message and stack trace) and the HAR file for failing network calls. Check the canary's IAM role and VPC settings if the failure is a timeout or an S3 access error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0192","card":"q-cloudwatch-061-reading-canary-results","sec":"cloudwatch-s062","q":"A canary passes, yet users report that the site is slow. How can the canary data help or fall short?","a":"<p>The <code>Duration</code> metric and the HAR timings show response times from the canary's location, so you can see a trend. They fall short for problems specific to user devices, networks or regions. Use RUM for real-user timing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0193","card":"q-cloudwatch-062-api-canary-with-two-steps","sec":"cloudwatch-s063","q":"Why would an API canary create an item with a POST and then fetch it with a GET in the same run?","a":"<p>A chain of calls tests a real workflow: write, then read back. It proves that data was stored and can be retrieved, which a single GET would not show. Each step is reported separately, so a failure points to the broken stage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0194","card":"q-cloudwatch-062-api-canary-with-two-steps","sec":"cloudwatch-s063","q":"In a CloudWatch Synthetics API canary, what does \"Continue if step fails\" control, and why turn it off for a create step?","a":"<p>It decides whether the canary runs later steps after an earlier one fails. Turn it off for steps later ones depend on: if the POST fails, the follow-up GET would fail too, and its error would only add noise. With it off, the run stops at the first real failure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0195","card":"q-cloudwatch-062-api-canary-with-two-steps","sec":"cloudwatch-s063","q":"What does the CloudWatch Synthetics API canary blueprint need to know about an Amazon API Gateway API?","a":"<p>It needs the API, the stage (which becomes part of the URL path) and the HTTP requests with method, path, headers and body. With the API Gateway option the console fills in the hostname. Mind the stage name in the path for REST APIs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0196","card":"q-cloudwatch-063-api-canary-with-a-random-payload","sec":"cloudwatch-s064","q":"Why generate a random id or payload in a CloudWatch Synthetics API canary instead of using fixed data?","a":"<p>A fixed id can collide with data from earlier runs, hide caching, or make a create call fail the second time because the item already exists. A random value makes each run independent and tests the real write path. The GET step uses the same random id to confirm the write.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0197","card":"q-cloudwatch-063-api-canary-with-a-random-payload","sec":"cloudwatch-s064","q":"What should an API canary that creates data do about that data afterwards?","a":"<p>Clean it up, for example with a DELETE step, or write to a test tenant with expiry (such as a DynamoDB TTL). Otherwise a canary running every minute fills the database with test records and can distort reports.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0198","card":"q-cloudwatch-063-api-canary-with-a-random-payload","sec":"cloudwatch-s064","q":"In a CloudWatch Synthetics Node.js canary, how do you share a value, such as an id created in one step, between steps?","a":"<p>Keep it in a variable in the handler's scope, since all steps run in the same invocation. Generate it before the first step and use it in later requests. No external storage is needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0199","card":"q-cloudwatch-063-api-canary-with-a-random-payload","sec":"cloudwatch-s064","q":"Why is it better to keep hostnames, paths and credentials out of canary script code?","a":"<p>Hardcoded values force a script edit for each environment and risk leaking secrets. Use environment variables on the canary for hostnames and read secrets from AWS Secrets Manager or Parameter Store, with an IAM role allowing access. The same script can then run against test and production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0200","card":"q-cloudwatch-064-logging-request-and-response-bodies","sec":"cloudwatch-s065","q":"In CloudWatch Synthetics, what do these step options change, and what is the risk?","a":"<p>They add request headers, the request body and the response body to the step's logs and reports. This helps debug failures. The risk is that headers and bodies may contain tokens, personal data or other secrets, which would then be stored in logs and S3. Enable them temporarily or only for non-sensitive endpoints.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>config: {\n    includeRequestHeaders: true,\n    includeRequestBody: true,\n    includeResponseBody: true\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0201","card":"q-cloudwatch-064-logging-request-and-response-bodies","sec":"cloudwatch-s065","q":"Why does CloudWatch Synthetics not log HTTP request and response bodies by default?","a":"<p>Bodies can be large and can contain sensitive data. Logging them increases log volume and cost, and risks exposing information to anyone with access to the logs. The default keeps logs small and safe, and you opt in when you need to debug.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0202","card":"q-cloudwatch-065-canary-recorder-plugin","sec":"cloudwatch-s066","q":"What does the CloudWatch Synthetics Recorder do, and when is it better than writing a canary by hand?","a":"<p>The Recorder is a browser extension that records your clicks, typing and navigation in a user flow and produces a canary script. It is quick for a first version of a UI flow with many steps. A hand-written script is better when you need clean, maintainable code, parameters, or assertions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0203","card":"q-cloudwatch-065-canary-recorder-plugin","sec":"cloudwatch-s066","q":"What should you fix after you record a CloudWatch Synthetics canary with the Recorder?","a":"<p>Replace the hardcoded URL with a configurable one, rename generic step names so results are readable, add assertions to check outcomes (not just actions), replace brittle selectors such as position-based ones with stable ids, and remove any real credentials captured while recording.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0204","card":"q-cloudwatch-065-canary-recorder-plugin","sec":"cloudwatch-s066","q":"Why is it dangerous to record a CloudWatch Synthetics script while logged in with a real user's credentials?","a":"<p>The recorder captures typed text, so a password or token may end up in the script and then in source control or logs. Use a dedicated test account with minimal permissions, and read its secret at run time from Secrets Manager.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0205","card":"q-cloudwatch-066-recorded-script-and-step-naming","sec":"cloudwatch-s067","q":"In CloudWatch Synthetics (Node.js with Puppeteer), what is <code>executeStep</code> for and how does it differ from <code>executeHttpStep</code>?","a":"<p><code>executeStep</code> wraps any function, usually browser actions with Puppeteer's <code>page</code>, as a named step with its own timing, pass or fail result and screenshots. <code>executeHttpStep</code> is for plain HTTP requests and records the request and response. Use <code>executeStep</code> for UI flows and <code>executeHttpStep</code> for API checks.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>await synthetics.executeStep(&#x27;Set user to Canary&#x27;, async function () {\n    await page.type(&#x27;#userInput&#x27;, &#x27;Canary&#x27;);\n});</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0206","card":"q-cloudwatch-066-recorded-script-and-step-naming","sec":"cloudwatch-s067","q":"Why give each step in a CloudWatch Synthetics canary a descriptive name?","a":"<p>Step names appear in results, logs and per-step <code>SuccessPercent</code> and <code>Duration</code> metrics. A name like <code>Create session</code> shows at once what broke, while <code>step_1</code> forces you to read the script. You can also alarm on a single step.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0207","card":"q-cloudwatch-066-recorded-script-and-step-naming","sec":"cloudwatch-s067","q":"In CloudWatch Synthetics, what do these settings do?","a":"<p>They control when the canary captures a screenshot of the page: after each step that succeeds, and after each step that fails. Failure screenshots are the most useful for debugging. Disabling success screenshots reduces S3 storage.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>syntheticsConfiguration.setConfig({\n    screenshotOnStepSuccess: true,\n    screenshotOnStepFailure: true\n});</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0208","card":"q-cloudwatch-067-what-x-ray-gives-you","sec":"cloudwatch-s068","q":"What problem does AWS X-Ray solve, and how is it different from CloudWatch metrics and logs?","a":"<p>X-Ray traces a single request across many services, so you can see where time was spent and where it failed. Metrics show aggregate trends and logs show events per component. In a microservice system, neither shows the path of one request across components; a trace does.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0209","card":"q-cloudwatch-067-what-x-ray-gives-you","sec":"cloudwatch-s068","q":"In AWS X-Ray, what are a trace, a segment and a subsegment?","a":"<p>A trace is the full path of one request, identified by a trace ID. A segment is the work done by one service for that request. A subsegment is a finer unit inside a segment, such as a downstream call to DynamoDB or an HTTP request, or a block of your own code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0210","card":"q-cloudwatch-067-what-x-ray-gives-you","sec":"cloudwatch-s068","q":"In AWS X-Ray, what are annotations and metadata and when do you use each?","a":"<p>Annotations are key-value pairs that are indexed, so you can filter traces by them (for example user tier). Metadata are non-indexed data of any type for extra detail. Use annotations for what you will search by and metadata for what you only read.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0211","card":"q-cloudwatch-067-what-x-ray-gives-you","sec":"cloudwatch-s068","q":"How does AWS X-Ray decide which requests to trace, and why is sampling needed?","a":"<p>A sampling rule decides which requests are recorded. The default rule traces the first request each second and 5% of the rest. Sampling controls cost and overhead on high-traffic services, while still giving a representative set of traces. You can add rules that sample important routes more.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0212","card":"q-cloudwatch-067-what-x-ray-gives-you","sec":"cloudwatch-s068","q":"How does a trace ID travel between services in AWS X-Ray?","a":"<p>Through the <code>X-Amzn-Trace-Id</code> HTTP header. Each instrumented service reads the header, adds its segment to the same trace and passes the header on. If a service in the chain is not instrumented or drops the header, the trace breaks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0213","card":"q-cloudwatch-067-what-x-ray-gives-you","sec":"cloudwatch-s068","q":"Which AWS services can send traces to X-Ray, and what must you do to enable them?","a":"<p>Lambda, API Gateway, ECS and EKS, Elastic Beanstalk, SNS, SQS and others can send traces. Some need only a setting (Lambda active tracing, API Gateway stage tracing). Application code on EC2 or containers needs the X-Ray SDK or OpenTelemetry instrumentation plus the X-Ray daemon or collector.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0214","card":"q-cloudwatch-067-what-x-ray-gives-you","sec":"cloudwatch-s068","q":"How long does AWS X-Ray keep trace data?","a":"<p>30 days. For longer retention, export trace data you need elsewhere or use the logs and metrics that CloudWatch keeps.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0215","card":"q-cloudwatch-068-instrumenting-python-code","sec":"cloudwatch-s069","q":"In the AWS X-Ray SDK for Python, what does <code>@xray_recorder.capture('name')</code> do?","a":"<p>It wraps the function so its run is recorded as a segment or, when a segment already exists (as in Lambda with active tracing), as a subsegment with that name. It captures the timing and any uncaught exception. It saves writing <code>begin_subsegment</code> and <code>end_subsegment</code> by hand.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>@xray_recorder.capture(&#x27;my_lambda_function&#x27;)\ndef lambda_handler(event, context):\n    ...</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0216","card":"q-cloudwatch-068-instrumenting-python-code","sec":"cloudwatch-s069","q":"In the AWS X-Ray SDK for Python, why does the code call <code>end_subsegment()</code> in a <code>finally</code> block?","a":"<p><code>finally</code> runs on success and on error, so the subsegment is always closed. An unclosed subsegment is never sent and breaks the nesting of later ones. <code>record_exception</code> attaches the error and stack trace before the exception continues.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>subsegment = xray_recorder.begin_subsegment(&#x27;api_call&#x27;)\ntry:\n    result = perform_operation(event)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    xray_recorder.record_exception(e)\n    raise\nfinally:\n    xray_recorder.end_subsegment()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0217","card":"q-cloudwatch-068-instrumenting-python-code","sec":"cloudwatch-s069","q":"In the AWS X-Ray SDK for Python, how do you make calls made through <code>boto3</code> show up as subsegments?","a":"<p>Call <code>patch_all()</code> from <code>aws_xray_sdk.core</code> early at startup, before creating clients. It patches supported libraries (boto3/botocore, requests and others) so each AWS call becomes a subsegment with service, operation and timing. Without patching, only your own captured code appears.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0218","card":"q-cloudwatch-068-instrumenting-python-code","sec":"cloudwatch-s069","q":"In AWS Lambda with active tracing, why can you create subsegments with the X-Ray SDK but not start your own segment?","a":"<p>Lambda creates the function's segment itself. The SDK can only add subsegments to it, and starting your own segment there is not allowed. In a web server (such as Flask or Django) the SDK's middleware creates the segment for each request.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0219","card":"q-cloudwatch-068-instrumenting-python-code","sec":"cloudwatch-s069","q":"What does an X-Ray daemon do, and when do you need to run it?","a":"<p>The daemon listens on UDP port 2000, batches segments from the SDK and sends them to X-Ray. Lambda includes it. On EC2, ECS or on-premises you run it yourself, or use the AWS Distro for OpenTelemetry collector. Without it, segments have nowhere to go.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0220","card":"q-cloudwatch-068-instrumenting-python-code","sec":"cloudwatch-s069","q":"Should new applications use the X-Ray SDK or OpenTelemetry for AWS tracing?","a":"<p>As of 2025, AWS recommends OpenTelemetry (through the AWS Distro for OpenTelemetry) for new instrumentation because it is vendor-neutral and the specification is the industry standard. Per the AWS X-Ray developer guide, the X-Ray SDKs and daemon enter maintenance mode on February 25, 2026 (security fixes only), so existing code keeps working but new work should start on OpenTelemetry.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0221","card":"q-cloudwatch-069-demo-app-with-cloudformation","sec":"cloudwatch-s070","q":"What is AWS CloudFormation and why is it a good way to deploy a sample or test environment?","a":"<p>CloudFormation creates AWS resources from a template. The set of resources is a stack, which you create, update and delete as one unit. This makes an environment repeatable, and deleting the stack removes everything it created, which avoids leftover costs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0222","card":"q-cloudwatch-069-demo-app-with-cloudformation","sec":"cloudwatch-s070","q":"Why should you delete a CloudFormation stack when you finish using a test environment?","a":"<p>Resources such as EC2 instances, load balancers, NAT gateways and databases keep costing money while they exist. Deleting the stack removes all of them in the right order. Check that termination or retention policies on items like S3 buckets and databases do not leave resources behind.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0223","card":"q-cloudwatch-070-enabling-tracing-in-a-lambda-template","sec":"cloudwatch-s071","q":"In a CloudFormation template for AWS Lambda, what does <code>TracingConfig</code> with <code>Mode: Active</code> do?","a":"<p>It turns on X-Ray active tracing for the function. Lambda samples incoming requests and sends a segment for each traced invocation. The default mode is <code>PassThrough</code>, which traces only when an upstream service already sampled the request.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>TracingConfig:\n  Mode: Active</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0224","card":"q-cloudwatch-070-enabling-tracing-in-a-lambda-template","sec":"cloudwatch-s071","q":"What permissions does a Lambda function need to send traces to X-Ray?","a":"<p>Its execution role needs <code>xray:PutTraceSegments</code> and <code>xray:PutTelemetryRecords</code>. The managed policy <code>AWSXRayDaemonWriteAccess</code> grants both. Without them, the function runs but no traces appear.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0225","card":"q-cloudwatch-070-enabling-tracing-in-a-lambda-template","sec":"cloudwatch-s071","q":"Lambda tracing is enabled but no traces appear in X-Ray. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check that active tracing is on for the function (or that an upstream service sampled the request in <code>PassThrough</code> mode), that the execution role has the X-Ray write permissions, that the function is invoked, that the sampling rules do not exclude it, and that the console region and time range are right.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0226","card":"q-cloudwatch-070-enabling-tracing-in-a-lambda-template","sec":"cloudwatch-s071","q":"In a CloudFormation template for Lambda, what is the role of the execution role's <code>AssumeRolePolicyDocument</code>?","a":"<p>It is the trust policy that says who may assume the role. For Lambda the principal is <code>lambda.amazonaws.com</code> with <code>sts:AssumeRole</code>, so the Lambda service can use the role. The attached policies then define what the function can do.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0227","card":"q-cloudwatch-071-reading-the-service-map","sec":"cloudwatch-s072","q":"In the AWS X-Ray service map, what do the nodes and edges show?","a":"<p>Nodes are services or resources (a client, an API, a function, a table). Edges are the calls between them. Click a node for latency, request count and error statistics, and use the map to find which dependency is slow or failing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0228","card":"q-cloudwatch-071-reading-the-service-map","sec":"cloudwatch-s072","q":"In the AWS X-Ray service map, what do the circle colors around a node mean?","a":"<p>Green means successful requests, red means server faults (5xx), yellow means client errors (4xx), and purple means throttling (429). The ring is split by the share of each kind. A node that is mostly red points to a failing service.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0229","card":"q-cloudwatch-071-reading-the-service-map","sec":"cloudwatch-s072","q":"Your service map shows a node with a red ring. How do you find the cause?","a":"<p>Select the node and open its traces, filter to faulted traces, and open one. The timeline shows which subsegment failed, and the exception details give the error message and stack trace. Compare with logs from the same trace ID for more detail.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0230","card":"q-cloudwatch-071-reading-the-service-map","sec":"cloudwatch-s072","q":"How does the AWS X-Ray service map help during an outage compared with checking each service's dashboard?","a":"<p>It shows dependencies and where errors or latency begin, so you can follow a failure from the user-facing service to the dependency that caused it. A single dashboard shows only one component's view.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0231","card":"q-cloudwatch-072-reading-traces","sec":"cloudwatch-s073","q":"In an AWS X-Ray trace, what does the timeline show and how do you use it?","a":"<p>The timeline shows each segment and subsegment as a bar on a time axis. You can see order, duration, which calls ran in parallel, and where time went, such as a slow DynamoDB call or a long cold start. Failed segments are marked, and clicking one shows the exception.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0232","card":"q-cloudwatch-072-reading-traces","sec":"cloudwatch-s073","q":"How do you find the slowest requests in AWS X-Ray?","a":"<p>Use trace filter expressions, for example by response time greater than a number of seconds, or by service, URL or annotation. You can also group traces and look at the latency distribution, then open a trace from the slow end.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0233","card":"q-cloudwatch-072-reading-traces","sec":"cloudwatch-s073","q":"In AWS X-Ray, how can you find all traces for one user or order?","a":"<p>Add an annotation (for example a user or order id) with the SDK, since annotations are indexed. Then use a filter expression such as <code>annotation.userid = \"123\"</code>. Metadata is not searchable, so it would not work for this.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0234","card":"q-cloudwatch-072-reading-traces","sec":"cloudwatch-s073","q":"Why might an AWS X-Ray trace show a gap or a missing downstream service?","a":"<p>The downstream service is not instrumented, the trace header was not passed on, the request was not sampled there, or the call used a library the SDK did not patch. Check that every hop passes <code>X-Amzn-Trace-Id</code> and that sampling is consistent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0235","card":"q-cloudwatch-072-reading-traces","sec":"cloudwatch-s073","q":"In AWS X-Ray, how can you tell whether a failed request through Amazon API Gateway to a Lambda function failed in the function or in API Gateway itself?","a":"<p>Open the trace and compare the nodes. If the Lambda segment is marked as a fault (red) or has an exception, the function failed. If the Lambda segment is fine but the API Gateway segment shows the error, the problem is in API Gateway, such as a malformed response from the function, a permissions problem or a timeout. The service map colors show the same split: red for 5xx faults, orange for 4xx errors, purple for throttling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0236","card":"q-cloudwatch-072-reading-traces","sec":"cloudwatch-s073","q":"When would you add an extra subsegment in AWS X-Ray around your own code?","a":"<p>When a block of work, such as a heavy computation, a call that is not auto-instrumented, or a loop, accounts for time you want to see on the timeline. Add it with <code>begin_subsegment</code> or a <code>capture</code> decorator. Too many subsegments add overhead and clutter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0237","card":"q-cloudwatch-073-tracing-dynamodb-calls-through-boto3","sec":"cloudwatch-s074","q":"In the AWS X-Ray SDK for Python, what does a patched <code>boto3</code> DynamoDB call add to the trace?","a":"<p>It adds a subsegment named after the service (DynamoDB). The subsegment records the operation (such as <code>GetItem</code>), the table name, the region, the request ID, the HTTP status and the duration. That lets you see how much of a request's time was spent in DynamoDB and which call failed, without writing any timing code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0238","card":"q-cloudwatch-073-tracing-dynamodb-calls-through-boto3","sec":"cloudwatch-s074","q":"Why does the low-level <code>boto3.client('dynamodb')</code> need typed values such as <code>Key={'game_id': {'S': 'abc'}}</code>, and how does the <code>resource</code> interface differ?","a":"<p>The low-level client sends DynamoDB's wire format, where every attribute carries a type tag (<code>S</code> string, <code>N</code> number, <code>BOOL</code>, <code>M</code> map, and so on). Numbers are passed as strings, for example <code>{'N': '5'}</code>. The higher-level <code>boto3.resource('dynamodb').Table(...)</code> converts plain Python values for you. Use the client for full control, the resource for shorter code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0239","card":"q-cloudwatch-073-tracing-dynamodb-calls-through-boto3","sec":"cloudwatch-s074","q":"In the AWS X-Ray SDK for Python, how do you instrument only some libraries instead of calling <code>patch_all()</code>?","a":"<p><code>patch_all()</code> instruments every supported library that is installed. <code>patch()</code> takes a tuple of module names, so only those are instrumented. Use it when you want less overhead or fewer subsegments in the trace. <code>botocore</code> is the library under <code>boto3</code>, so patching it covers <code>boto3</code> calls.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from aws_xray_sdk.core import patch\n\npatch((&#x27;botocore&#x27;, &#x27;requests&#x27;))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0240","card":"q-cloudwatch-073-tracing-dynamodb-calls-through-boto3","sec":"cloudwatch-s074","q":"In the AWS X-Ray SDK for Python, when should <code>patch_all()</code> be called, and what happens to AWS calls made before it?","a":"<p>Call it once at import time, at the top of the module, before the code makes AWS calls. Calls made before the patch is applied are not recorded as subsegments, so they are missing from the trace. Calling it inside every handler invocation is unnecessary.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0241","card":"q-cloudwatch-073-tracing-dynamodb-calls-through-boto3","sec":"cloudwatch-s074","q":"In the AWS X-Ray SDK for Python, what happens to an exception raised inside a function decorated with <code>@xray_recorder.capture('name')</code>?","a":"<p>The SDK attaches the exception and its stack trace to the subsegment and marks it as a fault, then re-raises it so your normal error handling still runs. In the console the trace shows the error under Exceptions with the failing subsegment highlighted.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0242","card":"q-cloudwatch-074-x-ray-with-api-gateway-and-lambda","sec":"cloudwatch-s075","q":"Which two settings must be turned on to get a connected trace from an Amazon API Gateway REST API to a Lambda function?","a":"<p>Turn on X-Ray tracing for the API stage in API Gateway, and turn on active tracing for the Lambda function. Tracing is enabled per stage, so a new stage needs it too. Both services also need permission to send data, which Lambda gets through its execution role. As of 2025, X-Ray tracing is a REST API feature and is not available for HTTP APIs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0243","card":"q-cloudwatch-074-x-ray-with-api-gateway-and-lambda","sec":"cloudwatch-s075","q":"What is the difference between Lambda's <code>PassThrough</code> and <code>Active</code> tracing modes?","a":"<p><code>PassThrough</code> is the default. Lambda traces an invocation only if the upstream caller already sampled the request and sent a trace header. <code>Active</code> makes Lambda sample requests itself, so functions invoked from callers that do not send a header (for example direct invokes or schedules) still produce traces. Active tracing needs <code>xray:PutTraceSegments</code> and <code>xray:PutTelemetryRecords</code> on the execution role.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0244","card":"q-cloudwatch-074-x-ray-with-api-gateway-and-lambda","sec":"cloudwatch-s075","q":"How can an X-Ray trace help you see a Lambda cold start?","a":"<p>A Lambda invocation appears with an Initialization subsegment before the Invocation subsegment when the execution environment was just created. Warm invocations have no initialization part. Comparing traces shows how much latency cold starts add, which guides decisions such as provisioned concurrency or a smaller package.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0245","card":"q-cloudwatch-074-x-ray-with-api-gateway-and-lambda","sec":"cloudwatch-s075","q":"A Lambda function behind API Gateway returns <code>{'statusCode': 400, ...}</code> for bad input. How does that differ in the trace and in the Lambda <code>Errors</code> metric from an unhandled exception?","a":"<p>A returned 400 is a successful invocation for Lambda: the <code>Errors</code> metric does not count it, and the function segment is not a fault. API Gateway still reports a 4xx to the client, so the API node shows an error. An unhandled exception counts in <code>Errors</code>, marks the Lambda segment as a fault, and API Gateway typically returns a 5xx (502 for a proxy integration).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def lambda_handler(event, context):\n    if &#x27;key_id&#x27; not in event:\n        return {&#x27;statusCode&#x27;: 400, &#x27;body&#x27;: &#x27;missing key_id&#x27;}\n    ...</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0246","card":"q-cloudwatch-074-x-ray-with-api-gateway-and-lambda","sec":"cloudwatch-s075","q":"Why does the X-Ray service map show two nodes for one Lambda function?","a":"<p>One node represents the Lambda service (the invoke call and its queueing), and the other represents the function itself (its own code and subsegments). Splitting them lets you tell service-side delay from time spent inside your handler.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0247","card":"q-cloudwatch-075-lambda-handler-that-validates-and-record","sec":"cloudwatch-s076","q":"Why should a Lambda handler validate required input fields before calling DynamoDB?","a":"<p>Failing early returns a clear 4xx response and avoids a wasted downstream call. Without it, DynamoDB rejects the request with a <code>ValidationException</code>, which surfaces as a 5xx or a confusing error and counts as a server fault in monitoring. Clean 4xx versus 5xx separation also keeps error alarms meaningful: return 400 for caller mistakes, and let exceptions propagate (or return 5xx) only for your own failures, so the Lambda <code>Errors</code> metric reflects real service problems.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0248","card":"q-cloudwatch-075-lambda-handler-that-validates-and-record","sec":"cloudwatch-s076","q":"What is the pitfall in this DynamoDB write when the caller sends a number in <code>data</code>?","a":"<p>The attribute is tagged <code>S</code> (string), but a number is not a string, so the low-level client raises a validation error before sending the request. Convert the value (<code>str(value)</code>), use the <code>N</code> type with a string like <code>'5'</code>, or validate the type up front. The same applies to <code>key_id</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>dynamodb.put_item(\n    TableName=&#x27;Items&#x27;,\n    Item={\n        &#x27;key_id&#x27;: {&#x27;S&#x27;: event[&#x27;key_id&#x27;]},\n        &#x27;data&#x27;: {&#x27;S&#x27;: event.get(&#x27;data&#x27;, &#x27;default&#x27;)}\n    }\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0249","card":"q-cloudwatch-075-lambda-handler-that-validates-and-record","sec":"cloudwatch-s076","q":"Why use <code>event.get('data', 'default')</code> rather than <code>event['data']</code> for an optional field?","a":"<p><code>dict.get</code> returns a default when the key is missing, while <code>event['data']</code> raises <code>KeyError</code>. Use <code>get</code> for optional fields and direct indexing (after validation) for required ones, so a missing required field fails with a clear message instead of an accidental KeyError.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0250","card":"q-cloudwatch-076-what-rum-collects","sec":"cloudwatch-s077","q":"How does data get from a user's browser into CloudWatch RUM?","a":"<p>A small JavaScript client runs in the page and records telemetry such as page loads, JavaScript errors and HTTP failures. It sends batches of events to the RUM data plane API (<code>PutRumEvents</code>), using temporary credentials from an Amazon Cognito identity pool, because browsers do not hold AWS credentials. CloudWatch RUM then stores and visualizes the events.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0251","card":"q-cloudwatch-076-what-rum-collects","sec":"cloudwatch-s077","q":"Why does synthetic or server-side monitoring not replace real user monitoring?","a":"<p>Synthetic checks run from fixed locations on a schedule and server metrics only see requests that reach the server. They cannot see slow rendering in an old phone, a failing script in one browser, or users blocked by a network far from your region. RUM measures what real users on real devices and networks experience.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0252","card":"q-cloudwatch-076-what-rum-collects","sec":"cloudwatch-s077","q":"What are the limitations of CloudWatch RUM?","a":"<p>It only has data when real users load pages, so a quiet site or a new release with no traffic produces nothing. Events can be lost if the browser blocks the request or closes early, and sampling means you see a share of sessions. It is also client-side, so it shows symptoms and needs traces, logs and metrics on the server to find root causes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0253","card":"q-cloudwatch-076-what-rum-collects","sec":"cloudwatch-s077","q":"What privacy and cost levers does CloudWatch RUM give you?","a":"<p>You can turn cookies off (sessions then rely on less persistent identifiers), choose which telemetries to collect, and set a session sample rate so only a share of sessions are recorded. Billing is per event collected, so these settings control both cost and the amount of user data you hold.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0254","card":"q-cloudwatch-076-what-rum-collects","sec":"cloudwatch-s077","q":"How can CloudWatch RUM be connected to X-Ray traces?","a":"<p>Enable X-Ray tracing for the app monitor and set the web client's HTTP telemetry to add the trace header (<code>addXRayTraceIdHeader: true</code>). Browser requests then carry a trace ID, so a slow page can be followed into the backend services in the service map.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0255","card":"q-cloudwatch-077-script-tag-form-of-the-sdk","sec":"cloudwatch-s078","q":"When would you load the RUM web client with a script tag instead of installing <code>aws-rum-web</code> from npm?","a":"<p>Use the script tag for static or server-rendered sites without a build step, or for a quick start: you paste a snippet into the HTML. Use npm for apps with a bundler: the version is pinned in <code>package.json</code>, it is typed, and it ships inside your own bundle. The trade-off is that a script tag loads one more network request and depends on the external file being available.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0256","card":"q-cloudwatch-077-script-tag-form-of-the-sdk","sec":"cloudwatch-s078","q":"In a snippet that injects a script with <code>document.createElement('script')</code>, what does <code>s.async = true</code> do?","a":"<p>It lets the browser download the script without blocking HTML parsing, and run it when ready. Scripts inserted dynamically are async by default, but setting it explicitly makes the intent clear. The cost is that errors thrown before the script finishes loading are not seen by the monitoring client.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>var s = document.createElement(&quot;script&quot;);\ns.src = &quot;https://example.com/rum.js&quot;;\ns.async = true;\ndocument.head.appendChild(s);</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0257","card":"q-cloudwatch-077-script-tag-form-of-the-sdk","sec":"cloudwatch-s078","q":"Is it safe to put the RUM app monitor ID and identity pool ID in public front-end code?","a":"<p>Yes, they are not secrets. The identity pool's unauthenticated role should allow only <code>rum:PutRumEvents</code> on the specific app monitor, so anyone who copies the IDs can at most send events to it. Never put access keys in front-end code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0258","card":"q-cloudwatch-077-script-tag-form-of-the-sdk","sec":"cloudwatch-s078","q":"Why should a RUM snippet be placed early in the page <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>?","a":"<p>The client can only record what happens after it starts. Loading it first lets it capture early JavaScript errors and resource timing. Loading it late means those early events are missed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0259","card":"q-cloudwatch-078-creating-an-app-monitor","sec":"cloudwatch-s079","q":"Why does a CloudWatch RUM app monitor use an Amazon Cognito identity pool?","a":"<p>Browsers have no AWS credentials and must not hold long-lived keys. The identity pool gives each visitor temporary credentials for an IAM role. That role should allow only <code>rum:PutRumEvents</code> for this app monitor. You can create the pool during setup or bring an existing one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0260","card":"q-cloudwatch-078-creating-an-app-monitor","sec":"cloudwatch-s079","q":"Why should you lower the session sample rate for a busy production site in CloudWatch RUM?","a":"<p>RUM is billed per event, and the sample rate is the percentage of sessions recorded. Recording every session on a high-traffic site costs a lot and adds little statistical value. A lower rate (for example 10 percent) still gives reliable averages and percentiles, but small segments or rare errors may be missed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0261","card":"q-cloudwatch-078-creating-an-app-monitor","sec":"cloudwatch-s079","q":"How long does CloudWatch RUM keep event data by default, and how can you keep it longer?","a":"<p>By default RUM keeps raw events for 30 days. You can turn on copying events to a CloudWatch Logs log group, where you control retention and can query them with Logs Insights.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0262","card":"q-cloudwatch-078-creating-an-app-monitor","sec":"cloudwatch-s079","q":"In CloudWatch RUM, what do the performance, errors and http telemetries each collect?","a":"<p><code>performance</code> collects page load timing, web vitals and resource timing. <code>errors</code> collects uncaught JavaScript errors and unhandled promise rejections with stack traces. <code>http</code> collects fetch and XHR request results, so failing API calls are visible. Removing one removes that tab's data and reduces events and cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0263","card":"q-cloudwatch-078-creating-an-app-monitor","sec":"cloudwatch-s079","q":"Why set an application version such as <code>appVersion</code> on a RUM client?","a":"<p>Events are tagged with the version, so you can compare error rates and load times between releases and spot a regression right after a deploy. Update it on every release.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0264","card":"q-cloudwatch-079-wiring-rum-into-a-vue-app","sec":"cloudwatch-s080","q":"In the CloudWatch RUM web client, what do <code>appId</code>, <code>identityPoolId</code> and <code>endpoint</code> each do?","a":"<p><code>appId</code> ties the events to one app monitor. <code>identityPoolId</code> is the Cognito pool used to get temporary credentials for sending data. <code>endpoint</code> is the regional RUM service URL that receives the events. <code>telemetries</code> selects what is collected, and <code>appVersion</code> labels the release.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>const config = {\n    appId: &#x27;&lt;your-app-monitor-id&gt;&#x27;,\n    appVersion: &#x27;1.0.0&#x27;,\n    identityPoolId: &#x27;&lt;your-identity-pool-id&gt;&#x27;,\n    endpoint: &#x27;https://&lt;aws-rum-endpoint&gt;&#x27;,\n    telemetries: [&#x27;performance&#x27;, &#x27;errors&#x27;, &#x27;http&#x27;]\n};\nconst rum = new AwsRum(config);</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0265","card":"q-cloudwatch-079-wiring-rum-into-a-vue-app","sec":"cloudwatch-s080","q":"Where in a single-page application should the RUM client be created, and why?","a":"<p>Once, at application start-up, before the app mounts (for example in the entry file). Creating it early lets it observe the first page load and errors, and creating it once avoids duplicate events and multiple sessions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0266","card":"q-cloudwatch-079-wiring-rum-into-a-vue-app","sec":"cloudwatch-s080","q":"Why should RUM initialization be wrapped in try/catch?","a":"<p>Monitoring must never break the application. If the config is wrong or the client fails to start, the exception would otherwise stop your app from loading. Catch it, optionally log it, and carry on.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0267","card":"q-cloudwatch-079-wiring-rum-into-a-vue-app","sec":"cloudwatch-s080","q":"You added the RUM client but no data shows in CloudWatch. How do you troubleshoot?","a":"<p>Open the browser's network tab and look for requests to the RUM endpoint (<code>PutRumEvents</code>). A 403 points to the identity pool role or the app monitor ID. No request means the client did not start (a config error, an ad blocker, or the wrong domain). Also check the session sample rate, the time range, and that the page was loaded for a while, since events are sent in batches.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0268","card":"q-cloudwatch-079-wiring-rum-into-a-vue-app","sec":"cloudwatch-s080","q":"In a Node-based front end, why is <code>aws-rum-web</code> installed with npm rather than the whole AWS SDK?","a":"<p><code>aws-rum-web</code> is a small client purpose-built for browsers and handles batching, session handling and sending events. The full AWS SDK would add far more code to the bundle and would not collect page telemetry for you.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0269","card":"q-cloudwatch-080-performance-and-web-vitals","sec":"cloudwatch-s081","q":"What are the Core Web Vitals, and what do they measure?","a":"<p>As of 2024, they are Largest Contentful Paint (LCP, loading speed), Interaction to Next Paint (INP, responsiveness, which replaced First Input Delay in March 2024) and Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS, visual stability). Google's \"good\" thresholds are LCP up to 2.5 s, INP up to 200 ms and CLS up to 0.1. They affect user experience and search ranking.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0270","card":"q-cloudwatch-080-performance-and-web-vitals","sec":"cloudwatch-s081","q":"Why use percentiles such as p75 or p90 for page load time in RUM instead of the average?","a":"<p>Real user timings are skewed: a few very slow loads on bad networks vanish in an average. Percentiles show what a typical and a slow user feel. Web vitals are judged at the 75th percentile.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0271","card":"q-cloudwatch-080-performance-and-web-vitals","sec":"cloudwatch-s081","q":"What is the difference between Lighthouse and RUM for performance data?","a":"<p>Lighthouse is lab data: one controlled run on a simulated device and network, good for debugging and CI checks. RUM is field data from real users with real devices, networks and locations. Lab data explains why a page is slow, field data tells you how many users are affected.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0272","card":"q-cloudwatch-080-performance-and-web-vitals","sec":"cloudwatch-s081","q":"RUM shows poor LCP only for mobile users. What do you check?","a":"<p>Filter the performance data by device and browser to confirm the pattern. Then look at what the largest element is, usually a hero image: its size and format, whether it is lazy-loaded, whether it is served from a CDN, and render-blocking scripts or CSS. Compress and resize images, preload the key image, and defer non-critical scripts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0273","card":"q-cloudwatch-080-performance-and-web-vitals","sec":"cloudwatch-s081","q":"How do you reduce a high Cumulative Layout Shift?","a":"<p>Reserve space before content loads: set width and height (or an aspect ratio) on images and embeds, give ads and banners fixed slots, and avoid inserting content on top of existing content. Load web fonts in a way that avoids reflow.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0274","card":"q-cloudwatch-081-errors","sec":"cloudwatch-s082","q":"Which JavaScript errors does the CloudWatch RUM web client capture automatically, and which need a manual call?","a":"<p>It captures uncaught exceptions and unhandled promise rejections. Errors you catch with <code>try/catch</code> are not seen unless you report them with the client's <code>recordError</code> method. Report caught errors that matter, such as a failed payment, so they appear in the RUM errors view.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0275","card":"q-cloudwatch-081-errors","sec":"cloudwatch-s082","q":"Why are stack traces in production JavaScript errors often hard to read, and what is the fix?","a":"<p>Production bundles are minified, so names and line numbers refer to the compiled file. Build source maps and keep them private, then use them to translate the stack trace to the original source file and line. Do not publish source maps publicly unless exposing source code is acceptable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0276","card":"q-cloudwatch-081-errors","sec":"cloudwatch-s082","q":"How would you find out whether a JavaScript error affects all users or only one browser?","a":"<p>Open the error group in RUM and look at the browser, device and version breakdown, and compare the number of affected sessions with total sessions. If it only happens on one browser version, look for an unsupported API or polyfill. If it follows a release, compare <code>appVersion</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0277","card":"q-cloudwatch-081-errors","sec":"cloudwatch-s082","q":"Why does RUM group errors by message and page?","a":"<p>A single bug can fire thousands of times. Grouping lets you see how many sessions and users each distinct error affects, so you fix the most impactful one first rather than reading raw events.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0278","card":"q-cloudwatch-082-other-rum-tabs","sec":"cloudwatch-s083","q":"Why can the RUM HTTP requests view find problems that server-side logs miss?","a":"<p>It records failures from the user's side. That includes timeouts, CORS rejections and network errors that never reach the server, so there is no server log line for them. Server logs only show requests that arrived.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0279","card":"q-cloudwatch-082-other-rum-tabs","sec":"cloudwatch-s083","q":"What is a session in CloudWatch RUM, and why is it useful?","a":"<p>A session is the series of page views and events from one visitor in one visit. Opening a session shows the order of page views, errors and requests, which helps reproduce a user's problem. Session and user IDs come from cookies (when enabled) and the Cognito identity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0280","card":"q-cloudwatch-082-other-rum-tabs","sec":"cloudwatch-s083","q":"How would you use the RUM browsers and devices view to prioritize work?","a":"<p>Compare traffic share with error rate and load time per browser and device. Fix or test first the combinations with many users and poor results, and drop support work for combinations with almost no traffic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0281","card":"q-cloudwatch-082-other-rum-tabs","sec":"cloudwatch-s083","q":"Why might user journeys in a single-page application show little data in CloudWatch RUM?","a":"<p>A single-page app does not load new HTML on navigation, so a page view only appears when the client detects route changes (through the History API) or you set page IDs explicitly. Check that route changes are reported, otherwise the whole visit looks like one page.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0282","card":"q-cloudwatch-083-what-internet-monitor-does","sec":"cloudwatch-s084","q":"What problem does Amazon CloudWatch Internet Monitor solve?","a":"<p>When users report slowness or failures, the cause is often somewhere on the internet between them and your AWS resources, outside your own metrics. Internet Monitor uses AWS's global network measurements to show availability and performance for your users by location and network provider, so you can tell whether the problem is on your side or in the internet path, and act in minutes instead of days.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0283","card":"q-cloudwatch-083-what-internet-monitor-does","sec":"cloudwatch-s084","q":"How does CloudWatch Internet Monitor differ from RUM and from CloudWatch Synthetics?","a":"<p>Internet Monitor uses AWS network telemetry for the traffic between client networks and your AWS resources, so you do not instrument the app. RUM runs a script in the user's browser and measures page-level experience. Synthetics runs scripted checks from AWS locations. Internet Monitor is best for ISP- and location-level internet problems, RUM for page experience, Synthetics for proactive checks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0284","card":"q-cloudwatch-083-what-internet-monitor-does","sec":"cloudwatch-s084","q":"What are the two scores in CloudWatch Internet Monitor, and what does each mean?","a":"<p>The availability score is the percentage of traffic that has no availability problem (users can connect). The performance score is the percentage of traffic that does not suffer degraded round-trip time. Lower scores for a city or network mean users there are affected.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0285","card":"q-cloudwatch-083-what-internet-monitor-does","sec":"cloudwatch-s084","q":"Internet Monitor shows poor performance only for users on one internet provider in one country. What can you do?","a":"<p>You cannot fix the provider's network, but you can reduce the impact: put resources or caches closer to those users (CloudFront, another region), use AWS Global Accelerator or a better routing choice, set up an alert, and communicate the status to support. Without a cause on your side, a rollback or scaling will not help.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0286","card":"q-cloudwatch-083-what-internet-monitor-does","sec":"cloudwatch-s084","q":"What does CloudWatch Internet Monitor cost depend on?","a":"<p>It is billed by the number of monitored resources and by city-networks (a city plus an ISP) that receive traffic, so more traffic from more locations raises cost. You can limit it by monitoring a percentage of traffic or setting a maximum number of city-networks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0287","card":"q-cloudwatch-084-creating-a-monitor","sec":"cloudwatch-s085","q":"Which resource types can a CloudWatch Internet Monitor monitor be attached to?","a":"<p>Amazon VPCs, CloudFront distributions, Network Load Balancers and Amazon WorkSpaces directories. Choose the resources that your users connect to.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0288","card":"q-cloudwatch-084-creating-a-monitor","sec":"cloudwatch-s085","q":"Why would you set the traffic percentage of an Internet Monitor monitor lower than 100?","a":"<p>To control cost, because billing grows with the number of city-networks monitored. A lower percentage gives a representative sample for high-traffic applications, with the trade-off that small locations may not have enough data. Use 100 if you are unsure or need full coverage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0289","card":"q-cloudwatch-084-creating-a-monitor","sec":"cloudwatch-s085","q":"Why would you publish Internet Monitor measurements to Amazon S3?","a":"<p>The console shows aggregated scores, while S3 keeps the detailed measurements for long-term storage and your own analysis, for example with Athena. It is useful for reports and for correlating with other data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0290","card":"q-cloudwatch-084-creating-a-monitor","sec":"cloudwatch-s085","q":"You created an Internet Monitor monitor but see no data. What do you check?","a":"<p>First, that the monitored resource receives real traffic, since there is no data without it. Then wait, because first data takes minutes and useful data takes hours. Also check the monitor's status is active, that the traffic percentage is not tiny, and that the resource type is supported and in the same region you are viewing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0291","card":"q-cloudwatch-084-creating-a-monitor","sec":"cloudwatch-s085","q":"Why delete an Internet Monitor monitor after testing?","a":"<p>It is billed while it exists, based on resources and city-networks, so a forgotten test monitor keeps costing money. Delete it, or reduce its scope, when you no longer need it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0292","card":"q-cloudwatch-085-generating-traffic","sec":"cloudwatch-s086","q":"Why does Internet Monitor need traffic before it shows anything?","a":"<p>Its scores are computed from measurements of actual client connections to your resources. No clients means no measurements, so there are no scores or health events. A test is only meaningful when requests come from real client locations and networks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0293","card":"q-cloudwatch-085-generating-traffic","sec":"cloudwatch-s086","q":"What is missing from this traffic generator loop that can make it hang?","a":"<p>There is no timeout. <code>requests.get</code> waits indefinitely by default, so one stalled connection can freeze the script. Pass <code>timeout=</code> (for example <code>requests.get(api_url, timeout=5)</code>), and consider a <code>Session</code> to reuse connections.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>response = requests.get(api_url)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0294","card":"q-cloudwatch-085-generating-traffic","sec":"cloudwatch-s086","q":"Why does generating test requests from a single laptop give a poor test of Internet Monitor?","a":"<p>All requests come from one city and one ISP, so you only get data for that single location. To see variety you need traffic from different networks and places, for example canaries in several regions or real users.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0295","card":"q-cloudwatch-085-generating-traffic","sec":"cloudwatch-s086","q":"Why does a test traffic loop add a short <code>time.sleep</code> between requests?","a":"<p>To control the rate. Without a pause, a tight loop can overwhelm a small API, hit API Gateway or Lambda throttling, and produce errors that come from your test rather than from the network.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0296","card":"q-cloudwatch-086-reading-the-monitor","sec":"cloudwatch-s087","q":"What is a health event in CloudWatch Internet Monitor?","a":"<p>It is a recorded period when availability or performance for a location and network dropped under a threshold, with the affected traffic, the impact and the scores. Events can also be sent to Amazon EventBridge, so you can alert or automate on them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0297","card":"q-cloudwatch-086-reading-the-monitor","sec":"cloudwatch-s087","q":"Which Internet Monitor view would you use to find which ISP or city is causing a performance problem?","a":"<p>The historical explorer. Filter or group by location (country, subdivision, metro, city) or network provider, and look at availability, performance, round-trip time and bytes over time. Compare the affected group with the rest to see whether the problem is local.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0298","card":"q-cloudwatch-086-reading-the-monitor","sec":"cloudwatch-s087","q":"Traffic insights in Internet Monitor suggest moving a resource closer to where most users are. What do you weigh before acting?","a":"<p>The expected drop in round-trip time against cost, data residency rules, where your dependencies such as databases live, and the work to run in another region. A cache or CloudFront distribution may give most of the gain without moving the origin.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0299","card":"q-cloudwatch-086-reading-the-monitor","sec":"cloudwatch-s087","q":"Why is round-trip time a better measure than bandwidth for judging how users experience a web app?","a":"<p>Web requests are many small exchanges, so each round trip adds delay and the total time is dominated by latency rather than raw bandwidth. High round-trip time makes pages feel slow even on fast connections.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0300","card":"q-cloudwatch-087-internet-monitor-alarm-and-api","sec":"cloudwatch-s088","q":"In this alarm, what happens when it enters ALARM state, and what is missing?","a":"<p>Nothing is notified. <code>ActionsEnabled=True</code> only enables actions, and there is no <code>AlarmActions</code> list such as an SNS topic ARN. Add <code>AlarmActions=[topic_arn]</code> (and optionally <code>OKActions</code>) so the alarm sends a notification.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>cloudwatch.put_metric_alarm(\n    AlarmName=&#x27;InternetMonitorLatencyAlert&#x27;,\n    MetricName=&#x27;Latency&#x27;,\n    Namespace=&#x27;AWS/InternetMonitor&#x27;,\n    ActionsEnabled=True,\n    ...\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0301","card":"q-cloudwatch-087-internet-monitor-alarm-and-api","sec":"cloudwatch-s088","q":"In Boto3, how do you read the state of an Internet Monitor monitor?","a":"<p>Create the client with <code>boto3.client('internetmonitor')</code> and call <code>get_monitor(MonitorName=...)</code>. The response includes the monitor's configuration and its <code>Status</code>, for example <code>ACTIVE</code>. The same client has calls for health events (<code>list_health_events</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0302","card":"q-cloudwatch-087-internet-monitor-alarm-and-api","sec":"cloudwatch-s088","q":"How can you be notified when Internet Monitor opens a health event, besides alarming on a metric?","a":"<p>Internet Monitor publishes health events to Amazon EventBridge. Create an EventBridge rule that matches them and route it to SNS, Lambda or a chat tool. This reacts to the event itself, while a CloudWatch alarm reacts to a metric threshold.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0303","card":"q-cloudwatch-088-critical-application-metric-not-appearin","sec":"cloudwatch-s089","q":"A critical application metric is not appearing in CloudWatch. What do you check?","a":"<p>Verify the metric is being published, the IAM permission <code>cloudwatch:PutMetricData</code>, the namespace, and the agent or SDK configuration.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import boto3\nclient = boto3.client(&#x27;cloudwatch&#x27;, region_name=&#x27;us-east-1&#x27;)\nclient.put_metric_data(\n    Namespace=&#x27;MyApp&#x27;,\n    MetricData=[{\n        &#x27;MetricName&#x27;: &#x27;CriticalMetric&#x27;,\n        &#x27;Value&#x27;: 100.0,\n        &#x27;Unit&#x27;: &#x27;Count&#x27;,\n        &#x27;Dimensions&#x27;: [{&#x27;Name&#x27;: &#x27;InstanceId&#x27;, &#x27;Value&#x27;: &#x27;i-1234567890abcdef0&#x27;}]\n    }]\n)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Console: CloudWatch &gt; Metrics &gt; All metrics, search <code>MyApp</code>/<code>CriticalMetric</code>. If missing, check Systems Manager Parameter Store or EC2 for the agent config, and the role in IAM &gt; Roles.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0304","card":"q-cloudwatch-088-critical-application-metric-not-appearin","sec":"cloudwatch-s089","q":"How can you check from the CLI whether a custom metric exists, and why does <code>list-metrics</code> need the exact dimensions to find it?","a":"<p><code>list-metrics</code> returns the metric with its dimensions if data was received, so an empty result means CloudWatch has no such metric in that region and account. A metric is identified by namespace, name and the full set of dimensions. A query with a different or partial dimension set matches a different metric, so run <code>list-metrics</code> without <code>--dimensions</code> first and read the dimensions that exist. A successful <code>PutMetricData</code> call is the only way a metric appears.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws cloudwatch list-metrics --namespace MyApp --metric-name CriticalMetric</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0305","card":"q-cloudwatch-088-critical-application-metric-not-appearin","sec":"cloudwatch-s089","q":"A metric stopped receiving data weeks ago and no longer shows in the console search. Is the data deleted?","a":"<p>No. Metrics with no new data points in about the last two weeks are hidden from the console search and from <code>ListMetrics</code>, but the stored data remains until its retention ends (15 months) and can be retrieved with <code>GetMetricData</code> if you know the exact name and dimensions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0306","card":"q-cloudwatch-088-critical-application-metric-not-appearin","sec":"cloudwatch-s089","q":"How long can it take for a newly published custom metric to appear in CloudWatch?","a":"<p>Usually within a couple of minutes, but the first data point of a new metric or dimension combination can take a little longer to be listed. Wait a few minutes before assuming it failed, and check that the publish call returned no error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0307","card":"q-cloudwatch-089-delayed-ec2-metrics","sec":"cloudwatch-s090","q":"CloudWatch metrics for an EC2 instance arrive late. Why?","a":"<p>Check the agent configuration, network latency and the collection interval.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;metrics&quot;: {\n    &quot;namespace&quot;: &quot;CWAgent&quot;,\n    &quot;metrics_collected&quot;: {\n      &quot;cpu&quot;: {\n        &quot;measurement&quot;: [&quot;cpu_usage_active&quot;],\n        &quot;metrics_collection_interval&quot;: 10\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Update the <code>AmazonCloudWatch-agent</code> config in Systems Manager Parameter Store to lower <code>metrics_collection_interval</code>, then restart the agent:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sudo /opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/bin/amazon-cloudwatch-agent-ctl -a restart</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0308","card":"q-cloudwatch-089-delayed-ec2-metrics","sec":"cloudwatch-s090","q":"Why does lowering <code>metrics_collection_interval</code> in the CloudWatch agent not make the <code>AWS/EC2</code> metrics arrive faster?","a":"<p>The <code>AWS/EC2</code> metrics such as <code>CPUUtilization</code> come from the hypervisor at 5-minute intervals (basic monitoring) or 1-minute intervals (detailed monitoring), not from the agent. The agent only controls its own metrics, which go to the <code>CWAgent</code> namespace by default. To get 1-minute built-in metrics, enable detailed monitoring on the instance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0309","card":"q-cloudwatch-089-delayed-ec2-metrics","sec":"cloudwatch-s090","q":"In a CloudWatch agent configuration, where can you set the collection interval globally and per metric?","a":"<p>The <code>agent</code> section has <code>metrics_collection_interval</code> as the default for all metrics (60 seconds by default). Each metric plugin under <code>metrics_collected</code> can set its own <code>metrics_collection_interval</code>, which overrides the default for that plugin. Use short intervals only where you need them, because more data costs more.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0310","card":"q-cloudwatch-089-delayed-ec2-metrics","sec":"cloudwatch-s090","q":"Why can a CloudWatch alarm react a few minutes after a spike even when metrics arrive every minute?","a":"<p>An alarm evaluates whole periods, so a spike late in a period is only seen when the period closes. Metric ingestion also adds a short delay, and an alarm with several evaluation periods needs several breaching data points. Shorter periods and fewer evaluation periods react faster but are noisier.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0311","card":"q-cloudwatch-090-custom-metric-with-incomplete-data","sec":"cloudwatch-s091","q":"A custom metric shows incomplete data. What is the cause?","a":"<p>Check publishing frequency, application logs, throttling and timestamp accuracy, and handle publish errors.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import boto3\nclient = boto3.client(&#x27;cloudwatch&#x27;)\ntry:\n    client.put_metric_data(\n        Namespace=&#x27;MyApp&#x27;,\n        MetricData=[{&#x27;MetricName&#x27;: &#x27;CustomMetric&#x27;, &#x27;Value&#x27;: 50.0, &#x27;Unit&#x27;: &#x27;Count&#x27;}]\n    )\nexcept client.exceptions.InvalidParameterValueException as e:\n    print(f&quot;Error: {e}&quot;)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Console: look for gaps in <code>CustomMetric</code>, review application logs under Logs &gt; Log groups, and confirm <code>cloudwatch:PutMetricData</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0312","card":"q-cloudwatch-090-custom-metric-with-incomplete-data","sec":"cloudwatch-s091","q":"What timestamp range does CloudWatch <code>PutMetricData</code> accept?","a":"<p>Timestamps up to two weeks in the past and up to two hours in the future. Anything outside that range is rejected with an invalid parameter error. If you omit the timestamp, CloudWatch uses the time it receives the data. This matters when you backfill data or when a server clock drifts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0313","card":"q-cloudwatch-090-custom-metric-with-incomplete-data","sec":"cloudwatch-s091","q":"What is the problem with only catching <code>InvalidParameterValueException</code> when publishing a metric in Boto3?","a":"<p>Other failures are different errors. Throttling comes back as a <code>ClientError</code> with a throttling error code, and network problems raise connection errors, so they escape this handler. Catch <code>botocore.exceptions.ClientError</code> and check the error code, handle connection errors, and retry throttled calls with backoff.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>try:\n    client.put_metric_data(Namespace=&#x27;MyApp&#x27;, MetricData=[...])\nexcept client.exceptions.InvalidParameterValueException as e:\n    print(f&quot;Error: {e}&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0314","card":"q-cloudwatch-090-custom-metric-with-incomplete-data","sec":"cloudwatch-s091","q":"A custom metric has gaps because the application crashes between publishes. What is a good design?","a":"<p>Publish from a component that outlives the work (for example the CloudWatch agent with StatsD, or an EMF log line), aggregate in memory and flush on a timer and on shutdown, and alarm with <code>treat missing data</code> set to breaching if silence means trouble. Keeping the publishing path simple and asynchronous keeps the app from losing data when it fails.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0315","card":"q-cloudwatch-091-no-metrics-for-a-newly-launched-resource","sec":"cloudwatch-s092","q":"A new instance has no metrics. What now?","a":"<p>Make sure the CloudWatch agent is installed, the IAM permissions are right, and the namespace and region are correct.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sudo yum install amazon-cloudwatch-agent\nsudo /opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/bin/amazon-cloudwatch-agent-config-wizard\nsudo /opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/bin/amazon-cloudwatch-agent-ctl -a start</code></pre></div>\n<p>Console: check the namespace (for example <code>AWS/EC2</code>), run <code>AWS-ConfigureCloudWatchAgent</code> from Systems Manager &gt; Run Command, and attach <code>CloudWatchAgentServerPolicy</code> to the role.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0316","card":"q-cloudwatch-091-no-metrics-for-a-newly-launched-resource","sec":"cloudwatch-s092","q":"How would you make sure every new EC2 instance in an Auto Scaling group sends CloudWatch agent metrics from the start?","a":"<p>Put the setup in the launch template: an instance profile with <code>CloudWatchAgentServerPolicy</code>, and either an AMI with the agent installed or user data that installs it and starts it with the configuration from SSM Parameter Store. You can also use a Systems Manager State Manager association to install and configure the agent on all tagged instances. This avoids manual steps for each launch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0317","card":"q-cloudwatch-091-no-metrics-for-a-newly-launched-resource","sec":"cloudwatch-s092","q":"A new instance in a private subnet with no internet access has the CloudWatch agent installed but no metrics. What do you check?","a":"<p>Network reachability to the CloudWatch endpoints. Without a NAT gateway or an internet route, the agent cannot send data. Create VPC interface endpoints for <code>monitoring</code> (and <code>logs</code> for logs), with a security group that allows HTTPS from the instance, and, if you use them, endpoints for SSM.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0318","card":"q-cloudwatch-091-no-metrics-for-a-newly-launched-resource","sec":"cloudwatch-s092","q":"Which metrics should appear for a new EC2 instance without any setup?","a":"<p>The <code>AWS/EC2</code> basic metrics: CPU utilization, network in and out, disk I/O for instance store volumes, and status checks. They appear a few minutes after launch. Memory and disk space usage are not in the list and need the CloudWatch agent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0319","card":"q-cloudwatch-092-metric-data-missing-for-certain-periods","sec":"cloudwatch-s093","q":"Metric data has gaps. What explains them?","a":"<p>Application downtime, publishing failures, API throttling or the retention period.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws cloudwatch list-metrics --namespace MyApp\naws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics --namespace MyApp --metric-name MyMetric --start-time 2025-07-01T00:00:00Z --end-time 2025-07-02T00:00:00Z --period 300 --statistics Average</code></pre></div>\n<p>Console: adjust the time range to find the gaps, and check application logs for downtime.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0320","card":"q-cloudwatch-092-metric-data-missing-for-certain-periods","sec":"cloudwatch-s093","q":"Why does a <code>get-metric-statistics</code> call with <code>--period 60</code> return nothing for a window 20 days ago?","a":"<p>CloudWatch keeps data at coarser resolution as it ages: 1-minute data is available for 15 days, then it is kept as 5-minute data for about 63 days, then 1-hour data. A request for 20 days ago must use a period of at least 300 seconds. The period must also be a multiple of 60.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0321","card":"q-cloudwatch-092-metric-data-missing-for-certain-periods","sec":"cloudwatch-s093","q":"Can you fill a gap in a CloudWatch metric by publishing old data later?","a":"<p>Yes, if the timestamps are within the last two weeks. <code>PutMetricData</code> accepts back-dated timestamps, so you can backfill a gap from a buffer or a log. Older data cannot be added. Alarms that already evaluated that period do not re-evaluate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0322","card":"q-cloudwatch-093-alarm-not-triggering","sec":"cloudwatch-s094","q":"An alarm does not fire although the threshold was breached. What do you verify?","a":"<p>The threshold, evaluation period, namespace, and SNS permissions.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws cloudwatch describe-alarms --alarm-names MyAlarm\naws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics --namespace MyApp --metric-name MyMetric --start-time 2025-07-01T00:00:00Z --end-time 2025-07-02T00:00:00Z --period 60 --statistics Average</code></pre></div>\n<p>Console: Alarms &gt; <code>MyAlarm</code> &gt; Details for the metric and threshold, Metrics to confirm the data really crosses the threshold, and confirm the SNS topic allows <code>sns:Publish</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0323","card":"q-cloudwatch-093-alarm-not-triggering","sec":"cloudwatch-s094","q":"How do you tell whether an alarm never entered ALARM state or entered it but failed to notify?","a":"<p>Read the alarm history (<code>aws cloudwatch describe-alarm-history --alarm-name MyAlarm</code>). It lists state changes and whether actions ran or failed. If there is no state change, look at the metric, statistic, period and threshold. If the state changed but there was no message, look at the action target, such as the SNS topic policy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0324","card":"q-cloudwatch-093-alarm-not-triggering","sec":"cloudwatch-s094","q":"An alarm uses the <code>Average</code> statistic, but the spike you care about lasts a few seconds. Why does it never fire?","a":"<p>Averaging over the period smooths the spike to a value under the threshold. Use <code>Maximum</code> (or a percentile such as p99) for spikes, or a shorter period. Pick the statistic that matches the question: <code>Sum</code> for counts, <code>Average</code> for steady utilization, <code>Maximum</code> or percentiles for latency tails.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0325","card":"q-cloudwatch-093-alarm-not-triggering","sec":"cloudwatch-s094","q":"What alarm setting can make an alarm change state without running its actions?","a":"<p><code>ActionsEnabled</code> set to false. The alarm still evaluates and changes state, but does nothing. It can be turned off with <code>disable-alarm-actions</code> (for example during maintenance) and left off by accident. Check it with <code>describe-alarms</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0326","card":"q-cloudwatch-094-frequent-false-positives","sec":"cloudwatch-s095","q":"An alarm produces many false positives. How do you tune it?","a":"<p>Increase the evaluation period, adjust the threshold, or use anomaly detection.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws cloudwatch put-metric-alarm --alarm-name MyAlarm --metric-name CPUUtilization --namespace AWS/EC2 --threshold 80.0 --comparison-operator GreaterThanThreshold --evaluation-periods 3 --period 300 --statistic Average --alarm-actions arn:aws:sns:us-east-1:123456789012:MyTopic</code></pre></div>\n<p>Console: Alarms &gt; <code>MyAlarm</code> &gt; Modify &gt; raise Evaluation Periods (for example 3) and adjust the Threshold.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0327","card":"q-cloudwatch-094-frequent-false-positives","sec":"cloudwatch-s095","q":"How does CloudWatch anomaly detection decide that a value is abnormal?","a":"<p>It trains a model on the metric's past data, including daily and weekly patterns, and builds an expected band around the metric. The alarm compares the metric with the band, with a width you choose in standard deviations. Values outside the band are anomalies, so the threshold adapts to seasonality instead of being a fixed number.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0328","card":"q-cloudwatch-094-frequent-false-positives","sec":"cloudwatch-s095","q":"In Boto3, how is an anomaly detection alarm defined?","a":"<p>The alarm uses a <code>Metrics</code> list instead of a plain metric name. One entry is the metric and another is the expression <code>ANOMALY_DETECTION_BAND(m1, 2)</code> (the 2 is the band width in standard deviations). <code>ThresholdMetricId</code> points at that band, and the comparison operator checks the metric against the upper or lower band. It also needs anomaly detection to be trained on the metric.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>cloudwatch.put_metric_alarm(\n    AlarmName=&#x27;CpuAnomaly&#x27;,\n    ComparisonOperator=&#x27;GreaterThanUpperThreshold&#x27;,\n    EvaluationPeriods=3,\n    Metrics=[\n        {&#x27;Id&#x27;: &#x27;m1&#x27;, &#x27;MetricStat&#x27;: {\n            &#x27;Metric&#x27;: {&#x27;Namespace&#x27;: &#x27;AWS/EC2&#x27;, &#x27;MetricName&#x27;: &#x27;CPUUtilization&#x27;},\n            &#x27;Period&#x27;: 300, &#x27;Stat&#x27;: &#x27;Average&#x27;}},\n        {&#x27;Id&#x27;: &#x27;ad1&#x27;, &#x27;Expression&#x27;: &#x27;ANOMALY_DETECTION_BAND(m1, 2)&#x27;},\n    ],\n    ThresholdMetricId=&#x27;ad1&#x27;,\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0329","card":"q-cloudwatch-094-frequent-false-positives","sec":"cloudwatch-s095","q":"Why alert on user-facing symptoms such as error rate and latency rather than only on causes such as high CPU?","a":"<p>High CPU is often harmless, so alarming on it produces false positives and alert fatigue. Symptom alarms fire when users are affected, and cause metrics are kept on dashboards for diagnosis. Keep a few cause alarms only where they predict a failure, like disk space running out.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0330","card":"q-cloudwatch-095-alarm-stuck-in-insufficient-data","sec":"cloudwatch-s096","q":"An alarm stays in INSUFFICIENT_DATA. Why?","a":"<p>The metric may not exist or has no data points, or the namespace or dimensions do not match.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics --namespace MyApp --metric-name MyMetric --start-time 2025-07-01T00:00:00Z --end-time 2025-07-02T00:00:00Z --period 60 --statistics Average</code></pre></div>\n<p>Console: check the metric in the alarm Details, verify data points under Metrics, and publish test data through the SDK or CLI.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0331","card":"q-cloudwatch-095-alarm-stuck-in-insufficient-data","sec":"cloudwatch-s096","q":"What state does a newly created CloudWatch alarm start in?","a":"<p>INSUFFICIENT_DATA. It changes to OK or ALARM after the first evaluation finds enough data. A new alarm on a metric with no data stays there, which is normal at first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0332","card":"q-cloudwatch-095-alarm-stuck-in-insufficient-data","sec":"cloudwatch-s096","q":"A low-traffic API's error count alarm keeps flipping to INSUFFICIENT_DATA. What setting fixes it?","a":"<p>Set <code>TreatMissingData</code> to <code>notBreaching</code>. The metric only has data when errors occur, so quiet periods are missing data, which by default (<code>missing</code>) leaves the alarm in INSUFFICIENT_DATA. <code>notBreaching</code> counts missing periods as healthy so the alarm goes to OK.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0333","card":"q-cloudwatch-095-alarm-stuck-in-insufficient-data","sec":"cloudwatch-s096","q":"In a CloudWatch alarm on a percentile such as p99, what does <code>EvaluateLowSampleCountPercentile</code> control?","a":"<p>It decides what happens when a period has too few samples for a reliable percentile. With <code>evaluate</code> the alarm still evaluates; with <code>ignore</code> it holds its state until enough samples arrive. Ignoring avoids false alarms from a single slow request on a quiet service.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0334","card":"q-cloudwatch-096-too-many-alarm-notifications","sec":"cloudwatch-s097","q":"Alarm notifications are too frequent. What do you change?","a":"<p>Lengthen the evaluation period or period, and adjust the threshold.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws cloudwatch put-metric-alarm --alarm-name MyAlarm --metric-name ErrorRate --namespace MyApp --threshold 10.0 --comparison-operator GreaterThanThreshold --evaluation-periods 5 --period 300 --statistic Average --alarm-actions arn:aws:sns:us-east-1:123456789012:MyTopic</code></pre></div>\n<p>Console: Alarms &gt; <code>MyAlarm</code> &gt; Modify &gt; set Evaluation Periods to 5 and Period to 300 seconds.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0335","card":"q-cloudwatch-096-too-many-alarm-notifications","sec":"cloudwatch-s097","q":"Does a CloudWatch alarm send a notification every period while it stays in ALARM?","a":"<p>No. Alarm actions run when the state changes (for example OK to ALARM, ALARM to OK), not on every evaluation. A flood of messages usually means the alarm is flapping between states, so make it less sensitive with a longer period, more evaluation periods, or an M-of-N setting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0336","card":"q-cloudwatch-097-sns-notifications-not-delivered","sec":"cloudwatch-s098","q":"SNS notifications do not reach recipients. What do you check?","a":"<p>The topic subscription, IAM permissions and endpoint validity.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws sns list-subscriptions-by-topic --topic-arn arn:aws:sns:us-east-1:123456789012:MyTopic\naws sns publish --topic-arn arn:aws:sns:us-east-1:123456789012:MyTopic --message &quot;Test message&quot;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Console: SNS &gt; Topics &gt; <code>MyTopic</code> &gt; Subscriptions to confirm the endpoint is confirmed, check the topic ARN on the alarm, and publish a test message from the SNS console.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0337","card":"q-cloudwatch-097-sns-notifications-not-delivered","sec":"cloudwatch-s098","q":"A CloudWatch alarm publishes to an SNS topic encrypted with the AWS managed key <code>alias/aws/sns</code>, and nothing is delivered. Why?","a":"<p>CloudWatch needs permission to use the key to publish, and the key policy of an AWS managed key cannot be edited to allow the CloudWatch service. Switch the topic to a customer managed KMS key whose key policy allows <code>cloudwatch.amazonaws.com</code> to use it (the data-key and decrypt actions).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0338","card":"q-cloudwatch-097-sns-notifications-not-delivered","sec":"cloudwatch-s098","q":"How do you find out why SNS failed to deliver a message to an HTTP endpoint or a Lambda function?","a":"<p>Turn on delivery status logging for the topic, which writes successes and failures to CloudWatch Logs, and attach a dead-letter queue (an SQS queue) to the subscription to keep messages that could not be delivered. Then check the endpoint's response codes and permissions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0339","card":"q-cloudwatch-097-sns-notifications-not-delivered","sec":"cloudwatch-s098","q":"What does the SNS topic access policy need for a CloudWatch alarm in the same account to publish?","a":"<p>A statement that allows the CloudWatch service principal (<code>cloudwatch.amazonaws.com</code>) to call <code>sns:Publish</code> on the topic, usually restricted with a condition on the alarm ARN or the account. Without it, the alarm's action fails and shows an error in the alarm history.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0340","card":"q-cloudwatch-097-sns-notifications-not-delivered","sec":"cloudwatch-s098","q":"Where do you see that an alarm action failed to run?","a":"<p>In the alarm's history, which records entries such as a failed action along with the reason. This confirms that the problem is in the delivery path, not in the alarm evaluation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0341","card":"q-cloudwatch-098-logs-not-appearing-in-cloudwatch-logs","sec":"cloudwatch-s099","q":"Application logs are not showing up. What do you verify?","a":"<p>Agent configuration, IAM permissions, and the log group and stream.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sudo cat /opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/etc/amazon-cloudwatch-agent.json\naws logs describe-log-groups --log-group-name-prefix my-app-logs</code></pre></div>\n<p>Agent log collection config:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;logs&quot;: {\n    &quot;logs_collected&quot;: {\n      &quot;files&quot;: {\n        &quot;collect_list&quot;: [\n          {\n            &quot;file_path&quot;: &quot;/var/log/application.log&quot;,\n            &quot;log_group_name&quot;: &quot;my-app-logs&quot;,\n            &quot;log_stream_name&quot;: &quot;{instance_id}&quot;\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Required IAM statement:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;,\n  &quot;Action&quot;: [&quot;logs:CreateLogGroup&quot;, &quot;logs:CreateLogStream&quot;, &quot;logs:PutLogEvents&quot;],\n  &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:logs:region:account-id:log-group:my-app-logs:*&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Check the agent status:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sudo /opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/bin/amazon-cloudwatch-agent-ctl -m ec2 -a status</code></pre></div>\n<p>Also confirm the <code>my-app-logs</code> group exists and the role has <code>logs:PutLogEvents</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0342","card":"q-cloudwatch-098-logs-not-appearing-in-cloudwatch-logs","sec":"cloudwatch-s099","q":"Why would the CloudWatch agent send nothing from a log file that exists?","a":"<p>The agent may not have read permission on the file. On Linux it runs as root by default, but if <code>run_as_user</code> is set, that user must be able to read the file. Also check that <code>file_path</code> matches the real path (globs are allowed), that the file is being written (the agent reads new lines), and that the agent log shows no errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0343","card":"q-cloudwatch-098-logs-not-appearing-in-cloudwatch-logs","sec":"cloudwatch-s099","q":"In a CloudWatch agent log configuration, what can <code>log_stream_name</code> placeholders do, and why use <code>{instance_id}</code>?","a":"<p>Placeholders such as <code>{instance_id}</code>, <code>{hostname}</code>, <code>{local_hostname}</code> and <code>{ip_address}</code> are replaced by the agent, so each server writes to its own stream in the same log group. This avoids interleaving lines from different instances and makes it easy to find a host's logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0344","card":"q-cloudwatch-098-logs-not-appearing-in-cloudwatch-logs","sec":"cloudwatch-s099","q":"What is a least-privilege IAM design for an application that sends logs to CloudWatch Logs?","a":"<p>Create the log group ahead of time (with infrastructure as code and a retention period), and give the role only <code>logs:CreateLogStream</code> and <code>logs:PutLogEvents</code> on that group's ARN. Allow <code>logs:CreateLogGroup</code> only if you really need dynamic creation, because it lets the app create groups without retention and cost control.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0345","card":"q-cloudwatch-098-logs-not-appearing-in-cloudwatch-logs","sec":"cloudwatch-s099","q":"A Lambda function writes logs but the log group does not appear. What is the likely cause?","a":"<p>The execution role lacks permission to write logs. The managed policy <code>AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole</code> grants <code>logs:CreateLogGroup</code>, <code>logs:CreateLogStream</code> and <code>logs:PutLogEvents</code>. Add it to the role, and check that the function has run since the fix.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0346","card":"q-cloudwatch-098-logs-not-appearing-in-cloudwatch-logs","sec":"cloudwatch-s099","q":"Which log driver sends container output to CloudWatch Logs in Amazon ECS, and what permission does it need?","a":"<p>The <code>awslogs</code> log driver, configured in the task definition with a log group, region and stream prefix. The task execution role needs permission to create log streams and put log events, and the log group must exist unless the driver is told to create it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0347","card":"q-cloudwatch-099-duplicate-log-entries","sec":"cloudwatch-s100","q":"Log entries are duplicated. What is the usual cause?","a":"<p>Several writers send the same logs. Check for more than one agent process or an application logger that also ships logs, and keep a single stream.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sudo ps aux | grep amazon-cloudwatch-agent\nsudo /opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/bin/amazon-cloudwatch-agent-ctl -m ec2 -a stop</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JavaScript</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>const winston = require(&#x27;winston&#x27;);\nconst { CloudWatchLogs } = require(&#x27;winston-cloudwatch&#x27;);\nconst logger = winston.createLogger({\n  transports: [\n    new CloudWatchLogs({\n      logGroupName: &#x27;my-app-logs&#x27;,\n      logStreamName: &#x27;app-stream&#x27;,\n      awsRegion: &#x27;us-east-1&#x27;\n    })\n  ]\n});</code></pre></div>\n<p>Check <code>my-app-logs</code> for duplicate streams and review the application code or agent config.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0348","card":"q-cloudwatch-099-duplicate-log-entries","sec":"cloudwatch-s100","q":"Why should an application not both write logs to a file read by the CloudWatch agent and push the same logs directly with an SDK or logging library?","a":"<p>Each path delivers its own copy, so every event is stored twice, doubling ingestion cost and confusing queries. Choose one path: stdout or file plus the agent, or a direct logging transport.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0349","card":"q-cloudwatch-099-duplicate-log-entries","sec":"cloudwatch-s100","q":"Why can a retried <code>PutLogEvents</code> call produce duplicate log lines?","a":"<p>CloudWatch Logs does not deduplicate events. If a client times out after the service accepted the batch and then retries, the same events are written twice. Since 2023 sequence tokens are no longer required, so they do not protect against this. Handle duplicates in queries or by using unique IDs in the log line.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0350","card":"q-cloudwatch-099-duplicate-log-entries","sec":"cloudwatch-s100","q":"How can log file rotation cause duplicate entries with the CloudWatch agent?","a":"<p>If <code>file_path</code> uses a pattern that also matches rotated files, such as <code>app.log*</code>, the agent reads the renamed file again. Use a pattern that matches only the active file. Rotation that copies and truncates the file can also confuse position tracking, so prefer the rename-and-create method.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0351","card":"q-cloudwatch-099-duplicate-log-entries","sec":"cloudwatch-s100","q":"How do you check how many CloudWatch agent processes run on an instance, and why does it matter?","a":"<p>List processes (<code>ps aux | grep amazon-cloudwatch-agent</code>) or check the agent status. Two agent processes, for example one started by a script and one by systemd, both send the same logs. Run exactly one and manage it through the control script or systemd.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0352","card":"q-cloudwatch-100-delayed-log-data","sec":"cloudwatch-s101","q":"Log data arrives late. What do you do?","a":"<p>Check agent metrics and increase the buffer.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics --namespace AWS/Logs --metric-name IncomingLogEvents --dimensions Name=LogGroupName,Value=my-app-logs --start-time $(date -u -Iseconds -d &#x27;-1 hour&#x27;) --end-time $(date -u -Iseconds) --period 300 --statistics Sum</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;logs&quot;: {\n    &quot;logs_collected&quot;: {\n      &quot;files&quot;: {\n        &quot;collect_list&quot;: [\n          {\n            &quot;file_path&quot;: &quot;/var/log/application.log&quot;,\n            &quot;log_group_name&quot;: &quot;my-app-logs&quot;,\n            &quot;log_stream_name&quot;: &quot;{instance_id}&quot;,\n            &quot;buffer_duration&quot;: 5000\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sudo /opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/bin/amazon-cloudwatch-agent-ctl -m ec2 -a start</code></pre></div>\n<p>Look at <code>IncomingLogEvents</code> under AWS/Logs, then update the agent config on the instance.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0353","card":"q-cloudwatch-100-delayed-log-data","sec":"cloudwatch-s101","q":"In the CloudWatch agent configuration, how do you control how often log events are sent?","a":"<p>In the unified agent, the <code>logs</code> section has <code>force_flush_interval</code>, which is the maximum time in seconds that events are buffered before being sent. Its default is 5 seconds. A lower value lowers delay but sends more, smaller batches. <code>buffer_duration</code> is a setting of the older CloudWatch Logs agent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0354","card":"q-cloudwatch-100-delayed-log-data","sec":"cloudwatch-s101","q":"How can you measure the delay between a log event and its arrival in CloudWatch Logs?","a":"<p>Logs Insights exposes <code>@ingestionTime</code>, the time CloudWatch received the event, next to <code>@timestamp</code>, the time of the event. The difference is the delivery delay. Large gaps point at agent buffering, network problems, throttling or clock drift on the source.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Text</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>fields @timestamp, @ingestionTime, @message\n| sort @timestamp desc\n| limit 20</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0355","card":"q-cloudwatch-100-delayed-log-data","sec":"cloudwatch-s101","q":"Which CloudWatch metrics show log ingestion volume for a log group?","a":"<p>In the <code>AWS/Logs</code> namespace, <code>IncomingLogEvents</code> and <code>IncomingBytes</code>, dimensioned by <code>LogGroupName</code>. A drop means that the source stopped sending, a spike explains rising cost. Use <code>Sum</code> as the statistic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0356","card":"q-cloudwatch-101-logs-insights-query-returning-incomplete","sec":"cloudwatch-s102","q":"A Logs Insights query returns incomplete results. How do you fix it?","a":"<p>Verify the syntax and time range, and optimize the filters.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Logs</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>fields @timestamp, @message\n| filter @message like /ERROR/\n| sort @timestamp desc\n| limit 100</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws logs start-query --log-group-name my-app-logs --start-time $(date -d &quot;-1 hour&quot; +%s) --end-time $(date +%s) --query-string &#x27;fields @timestamp, @message | filter @message like /ERROR/&#x27;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Console: Logs &gt; Logs Insights &gt; select <code>my-app-logs</code>, widen the time range and refine the filters.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0357","card":"q-cloudwatch-101-logs-insights-query-returning-incomplete","sec":"cloudwatch-s102","q":"In Boto3, why can <code>get_query_results</code> for a Logs Insights query return incomplete results?","a":"<p><code>start_query</code> is asynchronous. If you read the results right away, the status is still <code>Running</code> or <code>Scheduled</code> and the results are partial. Poll <code>get_query_results</code> until <code>status</code> is <code>Complete</code> (or <code>Failed</code>, <code>Cancelled</code>) before using the rows.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>q = logs.start_query(\n    logGroupName=&#x27;my-app-logs&#x27;,\n    startTime=start, endTime=end,\n    queryString=&#x27;fields @message | limit 100&#x27;,\n)\nresults = logs.get_query_results(queryId=q[&#x27;queryId&#x27;])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0358","card":"q-cloudwatch-101-logs-insights-query-returning-incomplete","sec":"cloudwatch-s102","q":"How many rows does a Logs Insights query return by default, and how do you get more?","a":"<p>As of 2025, a query without <code>limit</code> returns up to 10,000 events, and <code>| limit N</code> can raise that to 100,000. The <code>GetQueryResults</code> API returns at most 10,000 rows per call, so page with <code>nextToken</code>. For larger result sets, narrow the query with <code>filter</code> and <code>stats</code>, or export the data to S3.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0359","card":"q-cloudwatch-101-logs-insights-query-returning-incomplete","sec":"cloudwatch-s102","q":"Why does a Logs Insights query run over the wrong span with <code>start-query --start-time</code>?","a":"<p>The times are Unix timestamps in seconds, and the query only scans events within that range. A value in milliseconds is interpreted as a date far in the future, so the query finds nothing. Other APIs, such as <code>create-export-task</code>, use milliseconds, which is easy to mix up.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0360","card":"q-cloudwatch-101-logs-insights-query-returning-incomplete","sec":"cloudwatch-s102","q":"What happens when a Logs Insights query runs a long time?","a":"<p>Queries have a maximum run time (60 minutes). Large time ranges and many log groups slow a query and cost more because cost depends on data scanned. Narrow the time range and log groups, and filter early.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0361","card":"q-cloudwatch-102-log-groups-causing-storage-issues","sec":"cloudwatch-s103","q":"Log groups are growing and costing storage. What do you do?","a":"<p>Set a retention policy and export old data to S3.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws logs put-retention-policy --log-group-name my-app-logs --retention-in-days 7\naws logs create-export-task --log-group-name my-app-logs --from $(date -d &quot;-30 days&quot; +%s000) --to $(date +%s000) --destination my-s3-bucket --destination-prefix logs</code></pre></div>\n<p>Console: Logs &gt; <code>my-app-logs</code> &gt; Actions &gt; Edit retention (7 days), and Actions &gt; Export data to S3 with a time range and bucket.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0362","card":"q-cloudwatch-102-log-groups-causing-storage-issues","sec":"cloudwatch-s103","q":"What is the default retention for a new CloudWatch log group, and why is that a risk?","a":"<p>The default is never expire. Logs accumulate forever and storage cost grows silently. Set a retention period when you create the group, ideally through infrastructure as code, and archive older data to S3 if you need it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0363","card":"q-cloudwatch-102-log-groups-causing-storage-issues","sec":"cloudwatch-s103","q":"Can you set a CloudWatch log group retention of 10 days?","a":"<p>No. Retention uses a fixed list of values, for example 1, 3, 5, 7, 14, 30, 60, 90, 120, 150, 180 days, then 1, 13, 18 months, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 years, and never. Pick the closest allowed value.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0364","card":"q-cloudwatch-102-log-groups-causing-storage-issues","sec":"cloudwatch-s103","q":"How do an export task and a subscription filter with Firehose differ for archiving logs to S3?","a":"<p>An export task is a one-time, asynchronous copy of a time range of existing logs, and can take a while to complete. A subscription filter streams new events continuously to Kinesis, Firehose or Lambda, so you can deliver them to S3 near real time. Use export for backfills and subscriptions for ongoing archiving.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0365","card":"q-cloudwatch-102-log-groups-causing-storage-issues","sec":"cloudwatch-s103","q":"What usually costs more in CloudWatch Logs, ingestion or storage, and how do you reduce the bill?","a":"<p>Ingestion is typically the larger cost, charged per GB received, while storage per GB-month is much cheaper. Reduce it by logging less (appropriate log levels, no debug logs in production), filtering or sampling before sending, setting retention, and using the Infrequent Access log class for logs you rarely query.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0366","card":"q-cloudwatch-102-log-groups-causing-storage-issues","sec":"cloudwatch-s103","q":"In the AWS CLI, which units do <code>logs create-export-task --from</code> and <code>--to</code> use?","a":"<p>Milliseconds since the epoch. <code>start-query</code> for Logs Insights uses seconds. Using the wrong unit gives an empty or wrong time range.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0367","card":"q-cloudwatch-103-user-cannot-view-metrics","sec":"cloudwatch-s104","q":"A user cannot view CloudWatch metrics. What is missing?","a":"<p>IAM permissions such as <code>cloudwatch:GetMetricData</code> and <code>cloudwatch:ListMetrics</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;,\n  &quot;Action&quot;: [&quot;cloudwatch:GetMetricData&quot;, &quot;cloudwatch:ListMetrics&quot;, &quot;cloudwatch:DescribeAlarms&quot;],\n  &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;*&quot;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws iam get-user-policy --user-name user-name</code></pre></div>\n<p>Add the permission under IAM &gt; Users &gt; user-name &gt; Permissions, then test in CloudWatch &gt; Metrics.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0368","card":"q-cloudwatch-103-user-cannot-view-metrics","sec":"cloudwatch-s104","q":"Why does an IAM policy for reading CloudWatch metrics use <code>\"Resource\": \"*\"</code>?","a":"<p><code>GetMetricData</code>, <code>ListMetrics</code> and <code>GetMetricStatistics</code> do not support resource-level permissions, so they cannot be scoped to one metric ARN. You can still limit who gets them with the identity-based policy. The AWS managed policy <code>CloudWatchReadOnlyAccess</code> covers read access to metrics, alarms and dashboards.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0369","card":"q-cloudwatch-103-user-cannot-view-metrics","sec":"cloudwatch-s104","q":"A user has the right allow policy but still cannot see metrics. What else can block access?","a":"<p>An explicit deny in another policy, a permissions boundary, a service control policy in AWS Organizations, or a session policy. Use the IAM policy simulator, and check CloudTrail for the <code>AccessDenied</code> event, which names the denied action.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0370","card":"q-cloudwatch-103-user-cannot-view-metrics","sec":"cloudwatch-s104","q":"What does a user need to open CloudWatch dashboards beyond reading metrics?","a":"<p>Dashboard actions such as <code>cloudwatch:GetDashboard</code> and <code>cloudwatch:ListDashboards</code>. Metric read permission alone does not show saved dashboards.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0371","card":"q-cloudwatch-104-agent-not-sending-metrics","sec":"cloudwatch-s105","q":"The CloudWatch agent is not sending metrics. What do you check?","a":"<p>Agent status, configuration, and restart it.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sudo /opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/bin/amazon-cloudwatch-agent-ctl -m ec2 -a status</code></pre></div>\n<p>Edit <code>/opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/etc/amazon-cloudwatch-agent.json</code> (set the namespace, for example <code>CustomMetrics</code>, and <code>metrics_collected</code> such as <code>cpu</code> with <code>cpu_usage_active</code>), restart, and verify the namespace in CloudWatch &gt; Metrics:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sudo /opt/aws/amazon-cloudwatch-agent/bin/amazon-cloudwatch-agent-ctl -m ec2 -a start</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0372","card":"q-cloudwatch-104-agent-not-sending-metrics","sec":"cloudwatch-s105","q":"What does the error \"no valid providers in chain\" in the CloudWatch agent log mean?","a":"<p>The agent could not find AWS credentials. On EC2 it normally gets them from the instance profile through the instance metadata service. Check that a role is attached, that the metadata service is reachable (in containers the IMDSv2 hop limit may need to be 2), or configure a shared credentials profile for on-premises servers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0373","card":"q-cloudwatch-104-agent-not-sending-metrics","sec":"cloudwatch-s105","q":"Why should the agent's configuration be tested after a change with the agent log rather than only by looking at the console?","a":"<p>The console only shows data that arrived, and may show it late, so a bad config looks the same as a delay. The agent log shows parse errors, permission errors and send failures immediately, so you find the cause faster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0374","card":"q-cloudwatch-105-quota-blocking-a-new-alarm","sec":"cloudwatch-s106","q":"A quota limit prevents creating a new alarm. What do you do?","a":"<p>Check the quota and request an increase.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws service-quotas list-service-quotas --service-code cloudwatch\naws service-quotas request-service-quota-increase --service-code cloudwatch --quota-code L-12345678 --desired-value 1000</code></pre></div>\n<p>Console: Service Quotas &gt; AWS services &gt; Amazon CloudWatch &gt; Alarms &gt; Request quota increase.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0375","card":"q-cloudwatch-105-quota-blocking-a-new-alarm","sec":"cloudwatch-s106","q":"Are CloudWatch service quotas per region, and can all of them be raised?","a":"<p>Quotas such as the number of alarms apply per account and region. Many are adjustable through Service Quotas, but some are hard limits. In <code>list-service-quotas</code> the <code>Adjustable</code> field says whether you can request an increase.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0376","card":"q-cloudwatch-105-quota-blocking-a-new-alarm","sec":"cloudwatch-s106","q":"Before asking for a higher alarm quota, what alternatives would you consider?","a":"<p>Delete alarms that nothing reacts to, and replace per-resource alarms with fewer aggregate alarms using metric math (for example one alarm on the maximum across a fleet). Use composite alarms or anomaly detection where they reduce the count, and manage alarms through infrastructure as code so they do not pile up.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0377","card":"q-cloudwatch-105-quota-blocking-a-new-alarm","sec":"cloudwatch-s106","q":"How do you find the quota code to use in a Service Quotas request?","a":"<p>Run <code>aws service-quotas list-service-quotas --service-code cloudwatch</code>, or list in the console, and read the <code>QuotaCode</code> of the quota by name. Do not guess the code, then request the new value with <code>request-service-quota-increase</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0378","card":"q-cloudwatch-106-slow-dashboards","sec":"cloudwatch-s107","q":"CloudWatch dashboards load slowly. What helps?","a":"<p>Reduce widget complexity (fewer metrics per widget) and use longer periods.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;widgets&quot;: [\n    {\n      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;metric&quot;,\n      &quot;properties&quot;: {\n        &quot;metrics&quot;: [[&quot;AWS/EC2&quot;, &quot;CPUUtilization&quot;, &quot;InstanceId&quot;, &quot;i-1234567890abcdef0&quot;]],\n        &quot;period&quot;: 300,\n        &quot;stat&quot;: &quot;Average&quot;\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Console: Dashboards &gt; Edit widgets &gt; reduce metrics and raise the period (for example 300 seconds).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0379","card":"q-cloudwatch-106-slow-dashboards","sec":"cloudwatch-s107","q":"In a CloudWatch dashboard widget definition, what do the entries in the <code>metrics</code> array and the <code>period</code> and <code>stat</code> fields mean?","a":"<p>Each metric entry is a list: namespace, metric name, then dimension name and value pairs. <code>period</code> is the aggregation window in seconds, so a larger value returns fewer data points and loads faster. <code>stat</code> is the statistic, such as <code>Average</code>, <code>Maximum</code> or <code>p99</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;type&quot;: &quot;metric&quot;,\n  &quot;properties&quot;: {\n    &quot;metrics&quot;: [[&quot;AWS/EC2&quot;, &quot;CPUUtilization&quot;, &quot;InstanceId&quot;, &quot;i-1234567890abcdef0&quot;]],\n    &quot;period&quot;: 300,\n    &quot;stat&quot;: &quot;Average&quot;\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0380","card":"q-cloudwatch-106-slow-dashboards","sec":"cloudwatch-s107","q":"Why do dashboards with many widgets or many metrics per widget load slowly?","a":"<p>Each widget issues metric queries when the dashboard loads and refreshes, so every extra metric adds a call and more data points to render. Reduce the number of metrics per widget, use longer periods and shorter time ranges, and split large dashboards by purpose.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0381","card":"q-cloudwatch-106-slow-dashboards","sec":"cloudwatch-s107","q":"How do you build a dashboard widget for a fleet where instances come and go?","a":"<p>Use a Metrics Insights query or a <code>SEARCH</code> expression instead of a fixed list of instance IDs. For example, <code>SELECT AVG(CPUUtilization) FROM SCHEMA(\"AWS/EC2\", InstanceId) GROUP BY InstanceId</code> returns a series for each instance that exists, so new instances appear automatically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0382","card":"q-cloudwatch-106-slow-dashboards","sec":"cloudwatch-s107","q":"What is the difference between a metric math <code>SEARCH</code> expression and a Metrics Insights query?","a":"<p><code>SEARCH</code> finds metrics by name and dimension matching, then you graph them with a period and a statistic (it is limited in how many time series it shows, 500 per graph). Metrics Insights is a SQL-like query language that aggregates and groups across many metrics, such as the top 10 by average. Insights is better for large fleets and top-N views.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0383","card":"q-cloudwatch-107-high-api-request-latency","sec":"cloudwatch-s108","q":"CloudWatch API requests are slow. What do you do?","a":"<p>Check for throttling and enable metric streams.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics --namespace AWS/CloudWatch --metric-name ThrottledRequests --start-time $(date -u -Iseconds -d &#x27;-1 hour&#x27;) --end-time $(date -u -Iseconds) --period 300 --statistics Sum\naws cloudwatch create-metric-stream --name my-metric-stream --role-arn arn:aws:iam::account-id:role/my-stream-role --firehose-arn arn:aws:firehose:region:account-id:deliverystream/my-stream --output-format opentelemetry</code></pre></div>\n<p>Console: check <code>ThrottledRequests</code> under AWS/CloudWatch, then Metric streams &gt; create a stream with Kinesis Firehose.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0384","card":"q-cloudwatch-107-high-api-request-latency","sec":"cloudwatch-s108","q":"How should client code handle throttled CloudWatch API calls in Boto3?","a":"<p>Configure retries with exponential backoff and jitter. Boto3 supports the retry modes <code>legacy</code>, <code>standard</code> and <code>adaptive</code>; <code>adaptive</code> also slows the client down when it is throttled. Also reduce the call volume: request many metrics per <code>get_metric_data</code> call, cache results and lower the polling frequency.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import boto3\nfrom botocore.config import Config\n\ncloudwatch = boto3.client(\n    &#x27;cloudwatch&#x27;,\n    config=Config(retries={&#x27;max_attempts&#x27;: 10, &#x27;mode&#x27;: &#x27;adaptive&#x27;}),\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0385","card":"q-cloudwatch-107-high-api-request-latency","sec":"cloudwatch-s108","q":"How do you list a large number of metrics efficiently with Boto3?","a":"<p><code>list_metrics</code> returns results in pages, so use the paginator rather than only the first response. Filter by <code>Namespace</code>, <code>MetricName</code> or <code>Dimensions</code> to return fewer results. Without pagination, you silently see only part of the metrics.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>paginator = cloudwatch.get_paginator(&#x27;list_metrics&#x27;)\nfor page in paginator.paginate(Namespace=&#x27;MyApp&#x27;):\n    for metric in page[&#x27;Metrics&#x27;]:\n        print(metric[&#x27;MetricName&#x27;])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0386","card":"q-cloudwatch-108-high-metric-costs","sec":"cloudwatch-s109","q":"CloudWatch metric costs are high. How do you cut them?","a":"<p>Reduce collection frequency and stop publishing unused metrics.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;metrics&quot;: {\n    &quot;namespace&quot;: &quot;CustomMetrics&quot;,\n    &quot;metrics_collected&quot;: {\n      &quot;cpu&quot;: {\n        &quot;measurement&quot;: [&quot;cpu_usage_active&quot;],\n        &quot;metrics_collection_interval&quot;: 300\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Find high-frequency metrics under CloudWatch &gt; Metrics &gt; <code>CustomMetrics</code> and update the agent config on the instance. CloudWatch has no delete-metric API; a metric you stop publishing simply expires after its retention window, so stopping the publisher is what removes the cost.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0387","card":"q-cloudwatch-108-high-metric-costs","sec":"cloudwatch-s109","q":"Does changing the CloudWatch agent's collection interval from 60 seconds to 300 seconds reduce the custom metric charge much?","a":"<p>Not much. Custom metrics are billed per metric per month (each unique name, namespace and dimension set), not per data point. A longer interval mostly cuts the volume of data, not the metric count. The main savings come from publishing fewer distinct metrics and fewer dimension combinations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0388","card":"q-cloudwatch-108-high-metric-costs","sec":"cloudwatch-s109","q":"How do you find which part of your CloudWatch bill is growing?","a":"<p>Use Cost Explorer filtered to the CloudWatch service and group by usage type. This separates metrics, alarms, logs ingestion and storage, dashboards, API requests and Logs Insights queries. Then trace the largest one to the namespace or log group behind it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0389","card":"q-cloudwatch-108-high-metric-costs","sec":"cloudwatch-s109","q":"Can you delete a CloudWatch custom metric to stop paying for it?","a":"<p>There is no delete-metric API. Stop publishing it. The old data stays until it expires (15 months after the last data), but with no new data the metric stops being charged as an active metric. Fix the source, such as a dimension with unbounded values, so it does not come back.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"cloudwatch-f0390","card":"q-cloudwatch-108-high-metric-costs","sec":"cloudwatch-s109","q":"Which choices make a CloudWatch bill rise unexpectedly?","a":"<p>High-cardinality dimensions such as user IDs or request IDs (each value is a new metric), high-resolution metrics and alarms, detailed monitoring on many instances, verbose logs with no retention, frequent API polling, and many dashboards with custom metrics.</p>","hand":true}];
